/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:1:# Track C analysis: the factorial unit predicate
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:15:## 1. Baselines (instrumented)
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:79:## 2. Unit-only mechanisms: definitions and outcomes
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:112:**M6. Short-circuit group-law identities.** `x^K − 1`, Dickson/Lucas, division polynomials. See §3, O4: for `x^K − 1`, containing a long prefix forces `(p−1) | K` (`proved`, lemma below; known prior art: Pollard `p−1`). **Corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit:** the "(resp. the group order)" analogues for Dickson/Lucas and division polynomials are **not proved**. An integer prefix has no specified group-compatible embedding into a torus or a curve. Moreover, annihilating a point needs its **order**, not `#E(F_p)`, to divide `K` (e.g. on `y^2 = x^3+2x+3` over `F_101`, `#E = 96`, and the point `(3,6)` has order 12, so `12·P = O` while `96 ∤ 12`). Status of those analogues: `conjectured` / `not yet tested`.
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:116:## 3. Structural question: can a unit-only invariant have log-size dimension or degree?
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:124:**O1 (C-finite / finite-algebra powers).** `proved`.
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:136:- **Corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit (polynomial readouts).** For a polynomial readout of degree `D` of a dimension-`R` state, O1 bounds the **lifted dimension** `C(R+D, D)`, not `R`. Its hypothesis also needs zeros on `p < K ≤ p + C(R+D, D)`. The earlier claim "needs `R ≥ p+1`" is **wrong**. Counterexample, reproduced for `p ∈ {7, 11, 13, 31, 101, 257}`: `α` primitive in `F_{p^2}`, state `z_K = α^K` (`R = 2`), degree-4 readout `Norm(z − α^{p+1})·Norm(z − α^{p+2})`. It is nonzero for `1 ≤ K ≤ p` and zero at `K = p+1, p+2`. Interval version: state `(A, 1)` (`R = 2`) with readout `∏_{i<L}(A+i)` carries `I(A, A+L)` exactly (`p = 101, L = 7`). `audit_trackC.o1_poly_readout`.
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:140:**O2 (one power-map layer).** `proved`.
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:152:**O2′ (k layers, fibre bound).** `proved`, elementary. If `φ` is any composition of translations and power maps `y ↦ y^{e_i}`, its fibres have size `≤ ∏ g_i` with `g_i = gcd(e_i, p−1)`. So `#roots(Q) ≥ L / ∏ g_i`.
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:154:~~A large compression therefore needs exponents sharing a large factor with `p−1`.~~ **Corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit:** O2′ does **not** exclude large compression from many layers with bounded gcds. With `g_i ≤ G` the bound is `#roots(Q) ≥ L/G^k`, which becomes trivial at `k = ⌈log_G L⌉ = O(log L)` layers (e.g. `G = 2, L = 1000, k = 10`: bound `< 1`). With exponents independent of `p` such as `lcm(1..B)`, `g_i = gcd(lcm(1..B), p−1) = ∏_{ℓ ≤ B} ℓ^{min(v_ℓ(p−1), ⌊log_ℓ B⌋)}`, the truncated prime-power-smooth part. This is **not** always the `B`-smooth part: for `p = 257, B = 10` they are 8 and 256, and they differ for 536 of the 2 261 primes below 20 000. It is still Pollard `p−1` structure.
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:159:- ~~Status of a general k-layer bound beyond O2′: `conjectured` (`#roots(Q) ≥ c^{−k} L` for exponents with bounded gcd).~~ **Corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit:** in that form it is already `proved` by O2′ with `c = G` (`g_i ≤ G` ⇒ `#roots ≥ G^{−k} L`). The open question is a better constant, e.g. whether two gcd-2 layers can beat `3L/8`. The C-2R cycle (`notes/repair_C2R_results.md`) answers it for the tested family. Only the character exponent `E ≡ (p+1)/2` achieves it, and that exponent encodes `p−1`.
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:161:**O3 (bounded formal degree).** `proved`, trivial. If the test value is `P(A)` with `deg P < p`, then `deg P ≥ L`, because `P` needs `L` roots mod `p`. ~~Any escape needs formal degree `≥ p`.~~ **Corrected 2026-10-17 after r3 audit: WRONG.** O3 only forces degree `≥ L`, and degree is not circuit size. A hypothetical short circuit for the Pochhammer polynomial `∏_{i<L}(x+i)` has degree exactly `L < p` and the exact zero set `Z_L` (checked at `p = 101, L = 10`). Large formal degree is therefore *not* a necessary feature of an escape. The cheap sources of degree `≥ p` listed (power maps, algebra powers, group laws) remain the known ones.
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:163:**O4 (group-law identities contain long prefixes only via the group exponent).** `proved`.
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:173:- **Novelty calibration (r3 audit, verified 2026-10-17 against the primary source).** The `√p + 1` lemma is much weaker than known results. Martin–Parvardi, *Subproducts of small residue classes*, Canad. Math. Bull. 65(1) (2022) 1–8, Corollary 3: `y(p) ≪_ε p^{1/(4√e)+ε}`, where `y(p)` is the least `y` such that subset products of `{1,…,y}` cover `(Z/pZ)^*`. They also cite Burthe: the integers up to `O_ε(p^{1/(4√e)+ε})` generate the group. Either result shows that no proper subgroup contains `[1, L−1]` once `L ≫ p^{0.152}`. So O4's consequence holds at a much shorter prefix, and O4 is **not a new asymptotic obstruction**. Numerically, the least `y` with `⟨1..y⟩ = F_p^*` is at most 29 for all `p < 20 000`, against `√p ≈ 141` (`audit_trackC.generation_bound`).
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:177:- Their Theorem 2 (per `notes/prior_art_map.md`, not re-read by factC) says a polylog BSS decision procedure for `z ∈ {1, …, t}` makes `t!` ultimately easy. That is precisely the interval-zero-set problem that O1–O4 address in restricted models.
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:184:**What an escape must have.** All of the following, simultaneously. (Item 1 withdrawn 2026-10-17 after r3 audit: O3 gives only degree `≥ L`. Item 4 is proved only for `x^K − 1`.)
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:185:1. ~~formal degree `≥ p` (O3)~~ formal degree `≥ L` if the test is a single polynomial of degree `< p` (O3);
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:186:2. a state that is not a fixed-dimension power `M^K` (O1);
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:187:3. not a one-layer power map, and, if a composition of `k` power maps, not one with `∏ g_i ≪ L` (O2/O2′; `k = O(log L)` layers with bounded gcd are *not* excluded by O2′);
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:188:4. a zero set mod `p` that is additively ordered (an interval), not a union of cosets of a subgroup of some algebraic group (O4);
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md:193:The single most precise open question: **is there a family of polylog-size division-free SLPs over `Z[x]`, uniform in `L`, whose value at `x` is `0 mod p` iff `x mod p ∈ Z_L`, for every prime `p` in a dyadic range above `L`?** This is the mod-`p` form of Shub–Smale's Theorem 2.
