+# Hypothetical fast factoring: consequences, model escapes, and research targets
+
+Research round `polyimpl_1`, 19 October 2026. All computations and writes for this round were confined to the scratch directory. Repository statements below refer to the supplied notes, not to independently refereed results.
+
+**Status convention.** **KNOWN** identifies a checked published/preprint result, with its result number or, when the original has no number, an explicit unnumbered-result locator. **PROVED** supplies an argument here; it does not claim novelty. **SPECULATIVE** covers proposals, conjectural running times, and implications not established by the cited material. **FALSE** rejects a stated implication or overgeneralization; where an implication is merely unknown, it is called SPECULATIVE instead. Definitions and research recommendations are not assertions of established theorems. A label governs its statement and proof, including continuations around displayed formulas, until the next labelled statement or heading; subsidiary labels override it. Table labels govern their cells.
+
+## 1. Findings that change the research priorities
+
+1. **PROVED, §§2–3.** H1 makes the usual factorization-derived arithmetic functions deterministic polynomial time. H2 gives their randomized analogues. Neither assertion automatically removes randomness from every construction that uses a factorization: finding a general square root modulo a prime is a separate issue.
+2. **PROVED, §4.** The proposed short-interval/large-prime-factor route does **not** turn H1 or a positive-power H4 bound into a prime-construction exponent below $1/2$. Its exponent is $(\beta+\alpha)/\theta$, not $\beta\alpha$, and its prime generally lies in $[z,z^{1/\theta+o(1)}]$, not $[z,2z]$. All the checked interval theorems fail the necessary inequality $\beta+\alpha<\theta/2$.
+3. **SPECULATIVE.** No implication from H1 alone to deterministic polylogarithmic prime construction, new prime-gap bounds, the failure of the τ-conjecture, or easy prime-field discrete logarithms was established in this audit. These are not legitimate blockers without an additional reduction.
+4. **PROVED, §8.** A combined local/exposure barrier is possible: a T1-local collision search supplemented by a fixed list having at most $R$ prime-exposure signatures needs $X\gg N^{1/6}R^{-2/3}/\operatorname{polylog}N$ to factor every input in a balanced prime box. The signature restriction is essential; arbitrary group-order information is much stronger.
+5. **PROVED, §9.** A uniform oracle for $\varphi(N)\bmod m$ at sufficiently many small coprime moduli reconstructs the full totient by CRT. One fixed modulus, the ε bit, and squarefreeness are different oracle problems. Treating them as interchangeable hides the main difficulty.
+6. **SPECULATIVE, §10.** The most useful next work is a reduction/locality audit, followed by testing input-dependent selectors and relation-space transversality. Another generic correlation battery or another fixed-moment estimate has a relatively clear kill criterion already supplied by the repository.
+
+## 2. Hypotheses and quantitative transfer rules
+
+Write $n=\lfloor\log_2N\rfloor+1$ for the binary input length, and
+
+$$
+L_N[a,c]=\exp\!\big((c+o(1))(\log N)^a(\log\log N)^{1-a}\big).
+$$
+
+H1 means a uniform classical deterministic algorithm returning the complete prime factorization in $n^{O(1)}$ bit operations. H2 means a classical randomized algorithm with polynomial expected time, or polynomial bounded time and success probability at least $2/3$ on **each** input. H3 must specify both whether its bound is heuristic or proved and whether its quantifiers are worst case or distributional. H4 has separate meanings: all-input worst case, uniform balanced-semiprime average, or a specified exceptional-set bound. H5 specifies the oracle domain, allowed auxiliary arguments, error, and query sizes.
+
+**PROVED (certification).** A bounded-error full-factorization algorithm can be made Las Vegas with polynomial expected overhead: verify that the proposed prime powers multiply to $N$, certify primality, reject incorrect answers, and repeat. This uses deterministic polynomial-time primality testing, **KNOWN** by Agrawal–Kayal–Saxena, *PRIMES is in P*, Ann. Math. 160 (2004), **Theorem 1.1**, [paper](https://www.cse.iitk.ac.in/users/manindra/algebra/primality_v6.pdf).
+
+**PROVED (transfer rule).** Suppose a reduction makes $n^{O(1)}$ factorization calls on integers at most $N^c$, and otherwise costs $n^{O(1)}$. An $N^{\alpha+o(1)}$ factorizer gives time $N^{c\alpha+o(1)}$; an $L_N[a,C]$ factorizer gives $L_N[a,Cc^a]$, up to the largest query and polynomial factors. Queries only on integers at most $N$ preserve these bounds. The assertions follow by substitution and summation. A reduction polynomial in **bit length** need not preserve the numerical exponent if it increases query bit lengths by a polynomial power.
+
+| Hypothesis | Consequence justified by the transfer rule |
+|---|---|
+| H1 | **PROVED.** Every deterministic polynomial-time factorization-oracle reduction becomes deterministic polynomial time. A reduction with another randomized step remains randomized. |
+| H2 | **PROVED.** Polynomially many certified oracle calls give expected polynomial time on every input. This supplies no derandomization theorem. |
+| Rigorous H3 | **PROVED.** The exponent $a<1/3$, or $a=1/4$, transfers to reductions with $O(n)$-bit queries and polynomial overhead. It would improve the stated rigorous $L[1/2]$ frontier, and an exponent below $1/3$ would also improve the usual GNFS heuristic exponent. |
+| Heuristic H3 | **PROVED as a conditional implication.** The resulting algorithms inherit the same unproved assumptions. This does not establish a theorem about the density of smooth polynomial values. |
+| Worst-case H4 | **PROVED.** On the same inputs, multiplicative arithmetic functions below are computable in $N^{\alpha+o(1)}$. Uniform reductions must still account for their query sizes. |
+| Average/almost-all H4 | **PROVED.** A pointwise postprocessing algorithm inherits the source distribution and exceptions. Shifted, prime-power, discriminant, and short-interval queries do not inherit a balanced-semiprime guarantee without a separate distributional reduction. |
+| H5 | **PROVED.** No transfer is defined until the oracle specification is fixed. §§3 and 9 distinguish full values, residues, decisions, witnesses, and promises. |
+
+**PROVED (average is not worst case).** If a complete deterministic factorizer has mean cost $A(N)$ on a finite ensemble, Markov gives an exceptional fraction at most $A(N)/T$ at cutoff $T$. Conversely, an algorithm taking $N^a$ off an exceptional fraction $\eta$, with fallback $N^b$, has mean at most $N^a+\eta N^b$. For example, a mean $N^{1/6-\delta}$ gives exceptions at most $N^{-\delta/2}$ at cutoff $N^{1/6-\delta/2}$. An unspecified $o(1)$ exceptional fraction does not determine the mean exponent with a slow fallback.
+
+**KNOWN (baseline, not a lower bound).** Lenstra–Pomerance, *A rigorous time bound for factoring integers*, JAMS 5 (1992), pp. 483–516, **unnumbered main theorem in §1**, gives expected $L_N[1/2,1+o(1)]$ complete factoring. The method uses relations in quadratic class groups. The source theorem does not assume GRH. [Article](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0894-0347-1992-1137100-0).
+
+## 3. Q1(a): the computational-number-theory catalog
+
+### 3.1 Functions that follow directly
+
+**PROVED.** Given $N=\prod_i p_i^{e_i}$, the following values have $n^{O(1)}$-bit descriptions and can be computed with polynomial bit overhead:
+
+$$
+\begin{aligned}
+\omega(N)&=\#\{i\},&\Omega(N)&=\sum_i e_i,&d(N)&=\prod_i(e_i+1),\\
+\mu(N)&=\begin{cases}0&\exists i:e_i\ge2,\\(-1)^{\omega(N)}&\text{otherwise},\end{cases}\\
+\varphi(N)&=\prod_i p_i^{e_i-1}(p_i-1),&
+\sigma(N)&=\prod_i\frac{p_i^{e_i+1}-1}{p_i-1},\\
+\operatorname{rad}(N)&=\prod_i p_i,&
+\operatorname{sf}(N)&=\prod_{e_i\text{ odd}}p_i,&
+\operatorname{sq}(N)&=\prod_i p_i^{\lfloor e_i/2\rfloor}.
+\end{aligned}
+$$
+
+Here $\operatorname{rad}$ is the largest squarefree **divisor**, whereas $N=\operatorname{sf}(N)\operatorname{sq}(N)^2$. The formulas prove the claim; repeated squaring and integer arithmetic compute them. For fixed $k\ge1$, replace the σ factor by $(p_i^{k(e_i+1)}-1)/(p_i^k-1)$ to compute $\sigma_k$. Variable $k$ requires accounting for the output length.
+
+**PROVED.** Carmichael’s exponent follows by taking the lcm of the prime-power exponents: $\lambda(p^e)=p^{e-1}(p-1)$ for odd $p$, with $\lambda(2)=1$, $\lambda(4)=2$, and $\lambda(2^e)=2^{e-2}$ for $e\ge3$. For odd primes the unit group is cyclic; for powers of 2 it is $C_2\times C_{2^{e-2}}$. These descriptions prove the formula. H1 therefore computes λ as well.
+
+**PROVED.** Under H1, perfect-number, multiply-perfect-number, and amicable-pair recognition are in P: compute σ and test, respectively, $\sigma(N)=2N$, divisibility of σ by $N$, or the two defining aliquot-sum identities. No new existence theorem for odd perfect numbers follows from this recognition algorithm.
+
+### 3.2 Orders, totients, and divisor sums: the direction of reduction
+
+**PROVED (semiprime inversion).** For distinct primes $p,q$, one exact totient or divisor sum determines the factorization deterministically:
+
+$$
+s=p+q=N+1-\varphi(N)=\sigma(N)-N-1,
+\qquad p,q=\frac{s\mp\sqrt{s^2-4N}}2.
+$$
+
+The discriminant is $(p-q)^2$, proving both formulas and their polynomial overhead. This is stronger than the general randomized equivalences on this promise.
+
+**KNOWN.** Bach–Miller–Shallit, *Sums of divisors, perfect numbers and factoring*, SIAM J. Comput. 15 (1986), **Theorems 1 and 2**, proves factorization ⇒ σ in deterministic polynomial time and **one supplied value** σ ⇒ complete factorization in randomized polynomial time. Its **Theorem 3** supplies the splitting mechanism from a suitable multiple of $p-1$; §5 extends the σ result to fixed positive powers of divisors. This is a theorem about algorithms and supplied values, not an unconditional deterministic inverse for arbitrary $N$. [Author’s original](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~glmiller/Publications/Papers/BMS86.pdf), [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1137/0215083).
+
+**PROVED (multiple of λ ⇒ randomized splitting).** Given $M$ divisible by $\lambda(N)$, write $M=2^tu$, $u$ odd. For a random unit $a$, inspect $a^u,a^{2u},\ldots,a^{2^tu}=1$. Different 2-primary orders modulo two prime divisors produce a nontrivial square root of 1 and a factor by gcd. In a cyclic group of even order, the distribution of the 2-adic valuation of a random element’s order has every atom at most $1/2$. Independence under CRT makes the probability of different valuations at least $1/2$. Nonunits give a gcd immediately; prime powers can be detected and removed first. Repeat and recurse. The bit cost is polynomial in $\log N+\log M$. Supplying φ suffices because λ divides φ.
+
+**PROVED (factoring ⇒ order computation).** Factor $N$, form λ, factor λ, and repeatedly remove a prime divisor $r$ from a candidate exponent $E$ whenever $a^{E/r}=1\pmod N$. The final $E$ is exactly $\operatorname{ord}_N(a)$. Thus H1 gives deterministic polynomial-time order finding, with calls on integers at most $N$. Factoring only $N$, with no ability to factor numbers such as $p-1$, is a weaker supplied-data problem.
+
+**PROVED (order oracle ⇒ randomized factoring).** Use the exact order $r$ of a random unit $a$. If $r$ is even and $a^{r/2}\ne-1\pmod N$, take $\gcd(a^{r/2}-1,N)$. The same CRT 2-primary argument gives success probability at least $1/2$ for an odd integer with two distinct prime divisors. This proof establishes the randomized direction without invoking ERH.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (deterministic inverse, general inputs).** A general unconditional polynomial-time deterministic reduction from an exact φ-value oracle to factorization must not be inferred from the preceding random-base argument. Miller’s classical derandomization uses ERH; the semiprime promise has the elementary deterministic formula above. No claim that H1 needs to prove ERH is justified.
+
+**KNOWN (Miller’s exact conditional result).** Miller, *Riemann’s hypothesis and tests for primality*, JCSS 13 (1976), **Lemma 5 and Theorem 3 (ERH), p. 310**, proves deterministic polynomial equivalence of φ, its indicated exponent functions, and prime factorization under ERH. **Theorem 4 (ERH), p. 311**, treats rational-period/order computation with polynomial-time Turing reductions. The unconditional randomized proofs above separate which direction needs the hypothesis. [Author’s original](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~glmiller/Publications/Papers/Mi76.pdf).
+
+### 3.3 Quadratic residuosity, square roots, and two squares
+
+**PROVED.** Given the factorization, deciding whether $a$ is a square modulo $N$ is deterministic polynomial time. For each odd prime-power component, remove $p^{v_p(a)}$; the valuation must be even unless $a=0\pmod{p^e}$, and the remaining unit is a square exactly when Euler’s criterion modulo $p$ says so. For $2^e$, an odd unit is a square iff it is 1 modulo 8 when $e\ge3$, with the smaller exponents handled directly. Combine by CRT. In particular H1 puts quadratic-residuosity **decision** in P, including the Jacobi-$+1$ promise.
+
+**PROVED.** Square-root **construction** from a factorization is randomized polynomial time: choose a quadratic nonresidue modulo each odd prime by random sampling, use Tonelli–Shanks, Hensel lift, and CRT. Half the nonzero residues are nonresidues, and the remaining work is polynomial. For $p\equiv3\pmod4$, the root of a quadratic residue is $a^{(p+1)/4}$, without sampling.
+
+**SPECULATIVE.** H1 alone ⇒ a deterministic polynomial-time constructor for arbitrary $a,p$ was not established here. Factoring $p-1$ helps certify orders but does not by itself specify a deterministically found quadratic nonresidue. Consequently the statement “H1 makes every modular-root task deterministic polynomial time” is unsupported.
+
+**KNOWN, with a precise exception.** Schoof, *Elliptic curves over finite fields and the computation of square roots mod p*, Math. Comp. 44 (1985), **the unnumbered algorithm/results in §§3 and 4, pp. 486–494**, gives deterministic polynomial-time elliptic point counting, and deterministic polynomial time in $\log p$ for square roots of a **fixed integer**. The latter is not uniform polynomial time in $\log p+\log|a|$. In particular fixed radicand $-1$ is available. The paper states its results as algorithms rather than a numbered main theorem. [Paper](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~cs812-1/Schoof85.pdf).
+
+**PROVED (square-root search ⇒ factoring).** For odd squarefree $N$ with $r=\omega(N)\ge2$, sample a uniform unit $u$, ask a square-root oracle for a root $v$ of $u^2\bmod N$, and take $\gcd(u-v,N)$. Conditioned on $u^2$, the hidden $u$ is uniform over its $2^r$ CRT roots. Exactly two choices are $\pm v$, so success has probability $1-2^{1-r}$. The oracle must see the square, not the hidden $u$. This establishes randomized equivalence of general root search and factoring; it supplies no equivalent reduction from residuosity decision.
+
+**PROVED (two-square formula).** Let $r_2(N)=\#\{(x,y)\in\mathbb Z^2:x^2+y^2=N\}$, counting signs and order. Then
+
+$$
+r_2(N)=\begin{cases}
+0,&\text{some }p\equiv3\pmod4\text{ has odd }e_p,\\
+4\displaystyle\prod_{p\equiv1\ (4)}(e_p+1),&\text{otherwise}.
+\end{cases}
+$$
+
+Proof: in the Euclidean ring $\mathbb Z[i]$, a prime $3\pmod4$ stays prime, 2 ramifies, and a prime $1\pmod4$ splits into conjugates. A Gaussian integer of norm $N$ therefore exists exactly under the displayed parity condition. Each split prime permits $e_p+1$ allocations between the conjugates, and there are four units. This proves the criterion and count. Factoring plus fixed-$-1$ roots and Gaussian gcds also constructs **one** representation deterministically with polynomial overhead. Listing all representations has its own output cost.
+
+**PROVED (ε’s exact promise).** For distinct odd $p,q$:
+
+| Promise on $N=pq$ | Two-square decision and ε |
+|---|---|
+| $N\equiv1\pmod4$ | **PROVED.** $p\equiv q\pmod4$; $N$ is two squares iff both are 1 modulo 4, equivalently ε $=(-1/p)=+1$. Both 3 modulo 4 gives ε $=-1$. |
+| $N\equiv3\pmod4$ | **PROVED.** The primes have opposite classes. The answer to two-square decision is always “no”; ($-1/p$) depends on which prime is called $p$. |
+| $N=p^2$, odd prime $p$ | **PROVED.** The answer is always “yes”, whatever ($-1/p$). |
+
+The table follows directly from the parity criterion. Calling ε the two-square bit outside its first row is **FALSE**.
+
+### 3.4 Algebraic number theory, discrete logs, and constructions
+
+**KNOWN.** Buchmann–Lenstra, *Approximating rings of integers in number fields*, JTNB 6 (1994), **Theorem 1.3**, makes maximal-order **construction** polynomially equivalent to computing $\operatorname{rad}(d)$. **Theorem 1.2** constructs an overorder maximal at a supplied squarefree $m$; using $m=\operatorname{rad}(|\operatorname{disc}(A)|)$ suffices. **Theorem 6.12** makes maximal-order **recognition** equivalent to squarefreeness **decision**. **Theorem 1.4** relates the maximal order to the radical of the field discriminant. Thus H1 gives deterministic polynomial-time maximal orders. A squarefree-discriminant decision bit alone does not instantiate the construction theorem. [Paper](https://jtnb.centre-mersenne.org/item/10.5802/jtnb.113.pdf).
+
+**SPECULATIVE.** H1 ⇒ polynomial-time full class groups or fundamental units is not supplied by that theorem. Factoring the discriminant removes a local integrality obstacle; relation generation, saturation, principal ideals, and regulators remain separate computations. A proposed unit algorithm must specify ordinary versus compact output. Class-group factoring methods such as Schnorr–Lenstra, Seysen, and Lenstra–Pomerance do not constitute a reverse reduction from arbitrary class-group computation to integer factoring.
+
+**KNOWN (precise published direction).** Bach, *Discrete Logarithms and Factoring*, UCB/CSD-84-186 (1984), **numbered results 1–3 in the report’s abstract**, gives randomized polynomial reductions: composite-modulus DLP ⇒ factoring; prime-power DLP reduces to prime DLP; and general DLP reduces to factoring **plus prime DLP**. The accessible original is a scan; these are the source’s numbered abstract statements, not invented theorem numbers. [Report and numbered statements](https://www2.eecs.berkeley.edu/Pubs/TechRpts/1984/5973.html).
+
+**PROVED (why the first DLP direction works).** For random $a\in(\mathbb Z/N)^\times$, choose a uniform integer $t\in[0,N^2)$, compute $a^t$, and ask DLP for an exponent $e$ with $a^e=a^t$. Then $D=|t-e|$ is a multiple of $\operatorname{ord}(a)$. Conditioned on $a^t$, at most one possible hidden $t$ equals the oracle’s $e$, so $D=0$ has probability $O(1/N)$. Strip the power of 2 from $D$, and apply the root-of-unity chain to this same $a$. The CRT valuation mismatch still gives a factor. Oracle outputs must have polynomial bit length and depend only on the specified DLP inputs. Thus composite DLP supplies a randomized factorizer; H1 removes this factoring component while leaving prime DLP unsolved by the reduction.
+
+**PROVED (primitive-root certification versus finding).** If the distinct prime divisors of $p-1$ are known, $g$ is primitive iff $g^{(p-1)/r}\ne1\pmod p$ for each such $r$. The order must then contain every prime power dividing $p-1$. Random sampling finds a primitive root in expected $(p-1)/\varphi(p-1)\le1+\log_2p$ trials: if there are $k$ distinct prime divisors $r_i$, arrange them increasingly and use $r_i\ge i+1$, so $\prod_i(1-1/r_i)\ge1/(k+1)$. Under H1 this is a randomized polynomial-time construction. It is not a proof of deterministic polynomial-time primitive-root finding.
+
+**PROVED (point-counting exception).** For an elliptic curve with good reduction at every $p\mid N$,
+
+$$
+\#E(\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z)=\prod_{p^e\parallel N}p^{e-1}\#E(\mathbb F_p).
+$$
+
+CRT gives the product; smoothness gives $p$ lifts at each level of the one-dimensional curve. Factoring plus Schoof therefore computes this count with polynomial overhead, deterministically under H1. This does not change point counting over an already supplied prime field.
+
+**PROVED (limit on general point counting).** A polynomial-time point counter for arbitrary varieties of variable dimension over $\mathbb F_2$ would count satisfying assignments of Boolean formulas: use variables $x_i\in\mathbb F_2$, and for each clause set the product of its falsity indicators to zero. The number of common zeros is exactly #SAT. The modulus 2 is already factored. Thus a general point-counting collapse cannot be inferred merely by decomposing a composite modulus.
+
+**SPECULATIVE.** Analogous blanket claims about explicit expanders, finite-field generators, rational points, or special prime families need individual reductions. H1 makes it easier to certify multiplicative-order conditions and handle integer denominators; it does not automatically solve the search or distribution problem in each construction.
+
+## 4. Q1(b): prime construction, with the exponents kept separate
+
+### 4.1 What is unconditional, and what an oracle actually adds
+
+**KNOWN.** D.H.J. Polymath, *Deterministic methods to find primes*, Math. Comp. 81 (2012), pp. 1233–1246, **§1, pp. 2–3 of arXiv v4**, records the unconditional $z^{1/2+o(1)}$ construction via Lagarias–Odlyzko’s analytic prime-counting method. Its **Theorem 1.2** computes the parity of the prime count in intervals of length at most $z^{1/2+c}$ in $z^{1/2-c+o(1)}$ time. It does **not** establish the corresponding existence decision: a positive even count has parity zero. The paper’s **Conjecture 1.1** is the faster existence test. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1009.3956).
+
+**PROVED.** A factoring oracle answers, for a queried integer $m$, “which primes divide this $m$?” It does not find a successful $m$ in a large set, compute the product of that set without paying for it, or count successful queries. Deterministically scanning $K$ candidates and factoring each uses $K$ oracle calls. Binary search needs an interval-existence oracle, not just an individual factoring oracle.
+
+**PROVED (the literal size cap).** An oracle restricted to arguments $\le x$ cannot return a factor $>x$. Extracting a prime in $[x,2x]$ from a factored integer therefore needs a larger query, or another construction step. Replacing an argument cap $x$ by $2x$ preserves all fixed numerical exponents; that harmless convention still has to be stated. The conditional AKS scan below uses no factoring queries and so respects either cap.
+
+**PROVED (conditional polylog construction).** Assume a prime-gap upper bound $p_{j+1}-p_j\le C(\log p_j)^A$ for all sufficiently large primes, for fixed $A,C$. Test the next $O((\log z)^A)$ integers after $z$ with AKS. The first prime lies in $[z,2z]$ for large $z$, and the bit cost is polylogarithmic. Factoring is unnecessary. Cramér-type gaps imply such a bound; H1 does not prove the assumed gap estimate by this argument.
+
+**SPECULATIVE.** The analogous “factor consecutive integers” or “construct an integer with a large prime factor” proposal needs a guaranteed, efficiently searchable family. Neither the Polymath discussion nor H1 alone supplies such a family of polylogarithmic size whose factors land in $[z,2z]$. No unconditional polylogarithmic deterministic prime constructor is obtained here.
+
+### 4.2 A general reduction, including the output range
+
+**PROVED (interval extraction lemma).** Suppose an unconditional theorem states that every sufficiently large interval
+
+$$
+[X,X+X^{\beta+o(1)}]
+$$
+
+contains an integer $m$ having a prime factor $P^+(m)>X^\theta$, where $0<\theta\le1$. Suppose **all** integers of size $X^{1+o(1)}$ can be factored deterministically in $X^{\alpha+o(1)}$ time. Enumerating and factoring the interval finds such a prime in
+
+$$
+X^{\beta+\alpha+o(1)}.
+\tag{4.1}
+$$
+
+For a requested lower bound $z$, take $X=z^{1/\theta}$, with a harmless rounding/inflation if necessary. The prime satisfies
+
+$$
+z<p\le z^{1/\theta+o(1)},\qquad
+T(z)\le z^{(\beta+\alpha)/\theta+o(1)}.
+\tag{4.2}
+$$
+
+Proof: there are $X^{\beta+o(1)}$ queries, each of the indicated cost; verify every reported prime and select one above $X^\theta$. A divisor is at most the queried integer. Substitute $X=z^{1/\theta}$. For fixed positive ε in a source theorem, use its actual $\beta(\varepsilon),\theta(\varepsilon)$ before taking limits. In particular a limiting “(+o(1))” below means any fixed arbitrarily small exponent loss, unless uniform effectiveness in ε is separately established.
+
+**PROVED (necessary arithmetic for a blocker).** This recipe beats $z^{1/2+o(1)}$ only if
+
+$$
+\alpha<\theta/2-\beta.
+\tag{4.3}
+$$
+
+If $\beta\ge1/2$ and $\theta\le1$, its right side is nonpositive. Thus **even a unit-cost factoring oracle** cannot make this particular recipe break the square-root construction exponent using such a theorem. This is a theorem about the recipe, not a nonimplication theorem for all possible consequences of H1.
+
+### 4.3 The cited interval results
+
+**KNOWN.** The checked source statements are:
+
+- Baker–Harman–Pintz, *The difference between consecutive primes, II*, Proc. LMS 83 (2001), **Theorem 1**: $[X-X^{0.525},X]$ contains a prime for sufficiently large $X$. An interval immediately above $X$ of a constant multiple of this length follows by shifting the endpoint. [Author-uploaded original](https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Roger-Baker-2/publication/228991240_The_Difference_Between_Consecutive_Primes_II/links/53e14c630cf2235f35296d79/The-Difference-Between-Consecutive-Primes-II.pdf).
+- Merikoski, *Large prime factors on short intervals*, **Theorem 1**: interval length $X^{1/2}(\log X)^{b+\varepsilon}$, $b=1.388\ldots$, suffices for a factor $>X^{18/19}$. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.05123).
+- Jia–Liu, *On the largest prime factor of integers*, Acta Arith. 95 (2000), **unnumbered main Theorem, p. 17**: length $X^{1/2+\varepsilon}$, factor $>X^{25/26-\varepsilon}$. [Original](https://matwbn.icm.edu.pl/ksiazki/aa/aa95/aa9512.pdf).
+
+**SPECULATIVE (exact-number audit limitation).** Haugland’s *Application of Sieve Methods to Prime Numbers*, Oxford DPhil 1998, corrected April 2000, reports the earlier $24/25$ factor exponent in the **abstract, p. ii**. I located the author’s introduction but did not recover a numbered theorem from the main thesis. The Haugland row below is therefore a conditional arithmetic check of that reported exponent; the conclusion does not rely on it, since Jia–Liu is stronger. [Author’s thesis page](https://www.neutreeko.net/dphil.htm), [introduction](https://www.neutreeko.net/hauglandthesis_intro.pdf).
+
+In the following table, columns display the **proved arithmetic of (4.2)** applied to each stated input theorem. The ε losses in the Jia–Liu and Haugland rows are suppressed only in the displayed limiting exponents. $\alpha=1/6-\delta$ is a hypothetical **all-input** deterministic factoring bound here, not the repository’s balanced-semiprime average theorem.
+
+| Interval input $(\beta,\theta)$ | H1: $\alpha=0$ | $\alpha=1/6-\delta$ | $\alpha=1/5-\delta$ | Guaranteed prime range |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| **PROVED from BHP**, ($21/40,1$) | $21/40=0.525$ | $83/120-\delta=0.691667-\delta$ | $29/40-\delta=0.725-\delta$ | $z<p<z+O(z^{0.525})$ |
+| **PROVED from Merikoski**, ($1/2,18/19$) | $19/36=0.527778$ | $19/27-(19/18)\delta=0.703704-1.055556\delta$ | $133/180-(19/18)\delta=0.738889-1.055556\delta$ | $z<p\le z^{19/18+o(1)}$ |
+| **PROVED conditional arithmetic**, Haugland ($1/2+,24/25-$) | $25/48+=0.520833+$ | $25/36-(25/24)\delta+=0.694444-1.041667\delta+$ | $35/48-(25/24)\delta+=0.729167-1.041667\delta+$ | $z<p\le z^{25/24+o_\varepsilon(1)+O(\varepsilon)}$ |
+| **PROVED from Jia–Liu**, ($1/2+,25/26-$) | $13/25+=0.52+$ | $52/75-(26/25)\delta+=0.693333-1.04\delta+$ | $91/125-(26/25)\delta+=0.728-1.04\delta+$ | $z<p\le z^{26/25+O(\varepsilon)+o(1)}$ |
+
+**PROVED (interpretation).** Every displayed H1 exponent exceeds $1/2$. For a meaningful H4 power $\alpha\ge0$, decreasing α can do no better than the H1 column. The BHP algorithm is better implemented by testing primality, without factoring composites; it then costs $z^{0.525+o(1)}$ independently of α. One can always retain the existing $z^{1/2+o(1)}$ constructor instead of any inferior route. A factor of an integer just above $X$ need not exceed $X$; this is why the change from $X$ to $z$ is indispensable.
+
+**FALSE (the proposed blocker).** “Sub-$N^{1/6}$ factoring plus one of these interval theorems proves a new sub-square-root prime-finding bound.” Equations (4.1)–(4.3) refute that inference. A genuinely shorter interval theorem with $\beta+\alpha<\theta/2$, or a batched extraction algorithm that avoids scanning all integers, would change the calculation.
+
+**PROVED (distribution obstruction).** H4 on uniform balanced semiprimes cannot simply replace the all-input hypothesis in the interval lemma. Its queried integers need not be semiprimes, need not be balanced, and are not sampled from the theorem’s ensemble. An exceptional set with vanishing global density can contain a specified short interval: an interval of $X^\beta$ integers has density $X^{\beta-1}$ in a dyadic range. Thus an average theorem alone cannot certify success on these queries.
+
+### 4.4 Other prime routes and the actual conclusion
+
+**PROVED.** Factoring $\lfloor z\rfloor!+1$ yields a prime above $z$, but the input has $\Theta(z\log z)$ bits. H1 on explicit binary integers therefore gives only a polynomial-in-$z$ procedure from this construction. A short description of the integer is not a short binary input. Likewise, any use of $2^k-1$ must charge its $k$ input bits; an order congruence $p\equiv1\pmod k$ guarantees only $p\ge k+1$, not a $k$-bit prime.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (global conclusion).** The audit establishes **no new unconditional deterministic prime-construction bound from H1 or H4**. It does not prove that no such reduction exists. A reduction achieving one would itself be a useful new result; it is not presently an independently verified consequence to place in the blocker column.
+
+## 5. Q1(c): complexity, arithmetic circuits, and generic models
+
+### 5.1 Factorials and the τ-conjecture: keep all four notions apart
+
+Let $\tau(A)$ denote the minimum number of operations $+,-,\times$, starting from 1, that produce the integer $A$. For polynomials, variables are also available. Distinguish: existence of short programs; uniform construction of those programs; the exact factorial $k!$; and an arbitrary nonzero multiple of $k!$.
+
+**PROVED (uniform exact-factorial reduction).** If a deterministic algorithm constructs a ring-operation SLP for $k!$ in $(\log k)^{O(1)}$ time, then factoring is in FP. Evaluate the SLP modulo $N$. The predicate
+
+$$
+\gcd(k!\bmod N,N)>1
+$$
+
+is exactly $k\ge p_{\min}(N)$. Binary-search $1\le k\le\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$, after testing primality. At the threshold the integer $k$ is a prime divisor. There are $O(\log N)$ evaluations, all using polynomial-size residues. The same reduction works directly with a uniform modular-factorial evaluator.
+
+**PROVED (two cautions).** Existence of a short SLP does not supply its uniform constructor. Also, replacing $k!$ by an unspecified multiple $a_k k!$ invalidates the predicate: the multiplier may already contain a prime divisor of $N$ when $k<p_{\min}(N)$. Consequently the preceding proof cannot be applied to “ultimate complexity” without extra control of the multiples.
+
+**PROVED (a precise nonuniform version).** Polynomial-size Boolean circuits for the two-input function $(k,M)\mapsto k!\bmod M$, on binary inputs, give polynomial-size factoring circuits: unroll the polynomially many adaptive steps of the preceding algorithm. Mere bounds on individual integers $\tau$k!$$ do not supply that two-input circuit by this argument. A weaker consequence of $\tau$k!$\le(\log k)^{O(1)}$ is immediate: advise the SLP for $\lfloor3B/2\rfloor!$, then gcd its value with $pq$, for primes $p,q\in[B,2B]$. By the prime number theorem, a positive constant fraction of pairs straddle $3B/2$ and are split. The advice and the modular evaluation have polynomial size in $\log B$.
+
+**KNOWN (Lipton’s precise usable statement).** Lipton, *Straight-line complexity and integer factorization*, ANTS 1994, pp. 71–79, has the following explicitly numbered restatement in Koiran–Portier–Rojas, *Counting tropically degenerate valuations…*, **Theorem 1.14(II)**. If some ε $>0$ and sequence $f_j\in\mathbb Z[X]$ satisfy
+
+$$
+R_j:=\#Z_{\mathbb Z}(f_j)>\exp(\tau(f_j)^\varepsilon),\qquad
+\deg f_j,\ \max_{a\in Z_{\mathbb Z}(f_j)}|a|
+ \le2^{(\log R_j)^{O(1)}},
+$$
+
+then for infinitely many input lengths $n$, a fraction at least $n^{-O(1)}$ of $n$-digit products of two distinct equal-length primes have polynomial-size Boolean factoring circuits. This is a **nonuniform, infinitely-often, positive-fraction** conclusion, not uniform worst-case FP. Lipton’s original allows rational roots and mildly unequal prime lengths. [Explicit theorem and provenance](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1309.0486), [Lipton original](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-58691-1_45).
+
+**KNOWN.** The Shub–Smale τ-conjecture is $#Z_{\mathbb Z}(f)\le(1+\tau(f))^C$ for nonzero $f\in\mathbb Z[X]$, with one absolute $C$. It implies $P_{\mathbb C}\ne NP_{\mathbb C}$: Blum–Cucker–Shub–Smale, *Complexity and Real Computation*, **Theorem 3, p. 127**, also restated in **Theorem 1.14(I)** of the preceding source. H1 does not give a polynomial with too many integer roots, so it supplies no violation of this conjecture.
+
+**KNOWN.** Bürgisser, *On defining integers and proving arithmetic circuit lower bounds*, Computational Complexity 18 (2009), **Main Theorem 1.2** (numbered **Theorem 1.1** in the linked 2006 preprint), proves, in contrapositive form,
+
+$$
+\tau(\operatorname{Per}_n)=n^{O(1)}
+\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
+\tau$k!$=(\log k)^{O(1)}.
+$$
+
+The premise concerns constant-free, division-free arithmetic circuits. The theorem also treats the τ-conjecture and certain Taylor approximations. It does not have “integer factoring is easy” as its premise. [Published article](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00037-009-0260-x), [preprint with its own numbering](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2006/113/download).
+
+**KNOWN.** Cheng’s relevant title is *On the ultimate complexity of factorials*, STACS 2003 / TCS 326 (2004). **Theorem 1**, under **Conjecture 1** on smooth numbers in prime-centered Hasse intervals, constructs a nonzero multiple of $k!$ by an SLP of length $\exp(O(\sqrt{\log k\log\log k}))$, using randomized subexponential construction time. It does not prove that bound for exact $k!$, and its premise is not H1. [Author’s paper](https://qcheng2023.github.io/paper/factorial.pdf).
+
+**KNOWN (the separate ultimate-hardness implication).** Shub–Smale, *On the intractability of Hilbert’s Nullstellensatz and an algebraic version of “NP≠P?”*, Duke Math. J. 81 (1995), pp. 47–54, show that if there is no nonzero sequence $a_k$ with $\tau(a_k k!)=(\log k)^{O(1)}$, then $P_{\mathbb C}\ne NP_{\mathbb C}$. This is the **unnumbered displayed Shub–Smale result in Cheng §1, p. 2** of the linked primary paper. It is a hardness-to-separation implication; H1 does not establish its premise or its negation.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (reverse arrow).** No reduction H1 or H2 ⇒ short SLPs for exact factorials, the Pochhammer polynomial, or a violation of τ was established. The converse of “short factorial programs ⇒ easy factoring” is not obtained by reversing an implication. The meaningful blocker is restricted: an approach that **itself** constructs exceptionally short uniform product programs could resolve the stronger factorial/program problem, even though an arbitrary factorizer need not do so.
+
+### 5.2 Möbius circuits and dynamical disjointness
+
+**KNOWN.** Allender–Saks–Shparlinski, *A lower bound for primality*, JCSS 62 (2001), **Theorem 1** and its $\mathrm{AC}^{0}[p]$ consequence, shows that MAJORITY $\mathrm{AC}^{0}$-Turing-reduces to primality, squarefreeness, and gcd. In particular squarefreeness and primality are not in polynomial-size $\mathrm{AC}^{0}[p]$ for any prime $p$. These are bounded-depth, specified-gate-set lower bounds, not lower bounds against P. [Original preprint](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/1999/010/comment/1/download/).
+
+**PROVED.** An exact $\mathrm{AC}^{0}[p]$ circuit for the encoded values of μ would decide squarefreeness by testing μ $\ne0$, contradicting that restricted-model theorem. A polynomial-time μ algorithm gives circuits of polynomial size with potentially polynomial depth, and makes no such contradiction. Primality already illustrates compatibility between a P upper bound and an $\mathrm{AC}^{0}[p]$ lower bound.
+
+**KNOWN.** Green, *On (not) computing the Möbius function using bounded depth circuits*, CPC 21 (2012), **Theorem 1**, proves $\mathbb E_{m<2^n}\mu(m)F(m)=o(1)$ for fixed-depth, polynomial-size $\mathrm{AC}^{0}$ Boolean $F$. Bourgain, *On the Fourier–Walsh spectrum of the Möbius function*, **Theorem 1** in arXiv:1112.1423, bounds the low-level Walsh mass and gives the monotone-function application. Neither is a theorem excluding general polynomial-time evaluation of μ. [Green](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1103.4991), [Bourgain original](https://www.ias.edu/sites/default/files/math/csdm/11-12/jbourgain_on_the_fourier_walsh_coefficients.pdf).
+
+**SPECULATIVE (citation scope).** This audit does not upgrade Bourgain’s separately circulated $\mathrm{AC}^{0}[2]$ remark to a theorem about all polynomial-size parity-gate circuits. Nor does the general reference “Shparlinski’s books” identify an unrestricted circuit lower bound. A proposed circuit obstruction must specify depth, gates, uniformity, approximation measure, and input encoding.
+
+**PROVED (why H1 does not contradict Sarnak).** Polynomial time in the binary length of $m$ is not zero topological entropy under the shift $m\mapsto m+1$. For an explicit counterexample to that inference, concatenate, for $k=1,2,\ldots$, all $2^k$ binary words of length $k$ in lexicographic order. The $m$-th bit is computable in $(\log m)^{O(1)}$ time: locate its block of length $k2^k$, its word index, and its position in that word. Every finite binary word occurs; hence the shift orbit closure is the full binary shift, of entropy $\log2>0$. Therefore efficient evaluation does not put a sequence in Sarnak’s zero-entropy class. The conjecture’s disjointness assertion is not a computational hardness assertion for μ.
+
+### 5.3 What generic-model theorems actually exclude
+
+| Result and exact scope | Consequence for a fast factorizer |
+|---|---|
+| **KNOWN.** Aggarwal–Maurer, *Breaking RSA generically is equivalent to factoring*, EUROCRYPT 2009, **Theorem 1**: a successful generic-ring RSA inverter, using $+,-,\times,/$ and equality on hidden ring handles, yields an ordinary randomized factorizer with polynomial overhead. [Paper](https://crypto.ethz.ch/publications/files/AggMau09.pdf). | **PROVED.** This is a reduction to factoring. Once factoring is easy its hardness premise fails. It is not an unconditional lower bound on factoring in that model, much less on bit algorithms. |
+| **KNOWN.** Jager–Schwenk, *On the analysis of cryptographic assumptions in the generic ring model*, J. Cryptology 26 (2013), **Theorem 2** (same number in the ePrint), gives generic Jacobi evaluation ⇒ randomized factoring; **Theorem 3** treats quadratic residuosity. [Full version](https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/621.pdf). | **PROVED.** Ordinary Jacobi evaluation is already deterministic polynomial time by reciprocity and Euclid. This is direct evidence that the generic-ring restriction omits useful representation-dependent operations. It is not evidence that ordinary Jacobi bits are factoring-hard. |
+| **KNOWN.** Boneh–Venkatesan, EUROCRYPT 1998, **Theorem 3.1**: a straight-line reduction with at most $O(\log T)$ bounded-exponent radical calls and time $T$ can be converted to a $T^{O(1)}$ factorizer on its success set. [Original extended abstract](https://crypto.stanford.edu/~dabo/papers/no_rsa_red.pdf). | **PROVED.** The oracle-elimination theorem does not prove factoring hard or RSA easy. Its restrictions on algebraic operations, exponent sizes, and number of oracle calls cannot be dropped. |
+| **KNOWN.** Damgård–Koprowski, *Generic lower bounds for root extraction and signature schemes in general groups*, EUROCRYPT 2002, **Theorem 1 and Corollary 1**: random encodings and a hard distribution of hidden abelian-group orders give negligible generic strong-root success for polynomially many queries. [Original](https://www.iacr.org/archive/eurocrypt2002/23320252/DKea2002.pdf). | **PROVED.** A bit algorithm given an explicit integer modulus is not the hidden-order/random-encoding algorithm of the theorem. Supplying the order or its factorization changes its inputs and violates the hidden-information premise. No universal $N^{1/6}$ factoring exponent follows. |
+
+**PROVED (a modest unconditional straight-line floor).** Consider a fixed length-$s$ integer SLP starting from 1, independent of $N$, ending in a nonzero integer $V$. It succeeds only by $1<\gcd(V,pq)<pq$, for primes in ([B,2B]). Induction gives $\log_2|V|\le2^{O(s)}$. At most $\log|V|/\log B$ of those primes divide $V$. If $K$ is the number of primes in the interval, constant success on uniformly chosen distinct pairs requires $\Omega(K)$ divisible primes, hence $2^{O(s)}\gg K\log B\asymp B$. Therefore $s=\Omega(\log B)$. This is compatible with polynomial-time factoring. Brown’s *Lower bounds for straight line factoring* (2010), [original](https://eprint.iacr.org/2010/047.pdf), studies this fixed-program restriction; no exponential Brown bound is asserted here without its exact theorem.
+
+**FALSE.** “Every fast factorizer must violate generic-ring RSA–factoring equivalence” or “Shoup’s discrete-log lower bound proves a factoring lower bound.” The former is an equivalence, and the latter has a different task and representation. A fast algorithm only has to escape a lower-bound model **if its task and input information place it inside that model in the first place**.
+
+### 5.4 Pseudorandomness and natural proofs
+
+**KNOWN.** Razborov–Rudich, *Natural proofs*, JCSS 55 (1997), **Theorem 4.1**, relates a P/poly-natural property useful against P/poly to the absence of sufficiently hard pseudorandom generators. The hardness quantifier ranges over generators, not only factoring-based ones. [Original](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/tss/files/papers/1-s2.0-S002200009791494X-main.pdf).
+
+**PROVED.** H1/H2 falsify a polynomial-time factoring-hardness premise on any efficiently sampled distribution they cover. This invalidates the use of that premise to instantiate a factoring-based PRG/PRF security theorem. It does **not** refute the security reduction, prove that every generator is distinguishable, eliminate one-way functions based on other problems, or establish the hypotheses for a natural lower-bound proof. A false premise leaves an implication true.
+
+**PROVED (a concrete exposed-state BBS example).** For a Blum modulus $N=pq$, squaring permutes $QR_N$. Given $p,q$, its unique inverse on $QR_N$ is computable by exponentiation modulo $p,q$ and CRT, since $|QR_p|,|QR_q|$ are odd. Thus a generator output consisting of a final BBS state together with the successive extracted bits can be distinguished by reversing the states and checking the bits. This proves a break for that specified output format. It does not infer a converse to every security theorem about a stream with a completely hidden state.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (names and assumptions).** The commonly used Naor–Reingold prime-order PRF is based on DDH; it must be distinguished from factoring-based synthesizer constructions and Naor–Reingold–Rosen’s *Pseudo-random functions and factoring*. H1 does not solve prime-order DDH by the Bach reduction. An unsupported identification of all these assumptions would manufacture a complexity consequence.
+
+**PROVED (security scales).** With $n=\log_2N$, a positive-power H4 attack is $2^{\Theta(n)}$, and an H3 attack with fixed $a>0$ is $\exp(n^{a+o(1)})$. Neither is polynomial time. They therefore need not falsify the usual “no polynomial-time factoring adversary” assumption. H3 does rule out a claimed factoring security exponent strictly larger than its own attack exponent. These reductions therefore identify failed hardness premises, rather than contradictions with unconditional theorems.
+
+## 6. Q1(d–e): cryptography and analytic number theory
+
+**PROVED (cryptography; one paragraph).** H1 or H2 defeats RSA key recovery and Rabin inversion in polynomial time on every covered modulus: factor, compute φ/λ, and perform the appropriate root operations; it also defeats factoring/QR hardness premises in protocols and pseudorandomness constructions. Rigorous H3 changes asymptotic attack costs, while heuristic H3 gives only a heuristic attack; H4 remains slower asymptotically than the stated subexponential attacks, although it improves the deterministic guarantee. An average balanced-semiprime H4 result does not automatically cover another key distribution. H5 breaks a protocol only if its specified oracle suffices for the exact security task: an ε bit and the ordinary Jacobi symbol are not general key recovery. None of these conclusions collapses prime-field or elliptic-curve DLP merely by factoring an integer modulus.
+
+**PROVED (individual evaluations do not sum themselves).** H1 permits evaluating $\mu(m)$, representing $\Lambda(m)$ exactly as 0 or $\log p$, or deciding $P^+(m)\le y$ in polynomial time in $\log m+\log y$. Summing over $m\le X$ by this method still makes $X$ calls. Faster evaluation of each summand supplies no cancellation estimate, gap bound, zero-free region, or faster summatory algorithm. Primality itself is already efficiently decidable, so replacing that decision by factoring in a sequential scan is not a new prime-counting theorem.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (analytic consequences).** No new theorem about prime gaps, smooth-number density in a specified short interval, explicit Linnik constants, RH, or the infinitude of a prescribed prime pattern follows from the reductions audited here. Finite verification can become cheaper when its bottleneck is factoring; that is a statement about a finite computation, not an infinite conjecture. The exceptions are conditional algorithmic constructions where an **independent** existence theorem supplies an efficiently searchable family, as quantified in §4, and consequences of a **particular mechanism** proving a coverage theorem, as in §7. Those should be recorded as mechanism-dependent blockers.
+
+## 7. Q2: escape signatures, with the actual models exposed
+
+### 7.1 Repository models
+
+For this section $P\asymp\sqrt N$, $X$ is the charged search budget, and “covered” means covered by the certified target identity. An accidental collision is not a certified identity hit. Links point to the read-only source notes.
+
+| Barrier | Exact assumptions and conclusion | What a faster method must change; existing examples |
+|---|---|---|
+| Multimod **Theorem A**, A′, B | **PROVED in the stated model.** Fixed degree bound $d$; targets are valid Laurent/class-lock identities, each with its own modulus and residue class; finitely many interval windows; **all integer candidates** in a constant-ratio factor interval are covered; $\#\tau\le X$, $\sum W_\tau\le X^2$. Then $X\gg N^{1/6}$. Degree $\ge2$ targets alone require $N^{1/5}$; with $J$ moduli the refined bound is $N^{1/5-o(1)}J^{-1/5}$. [§§1, 3.1–3.3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/multimod_results.md). | **PROVED by contraposition.** A sub-$1/6$ method cannot retain these coverage and cost assumptions. Relation methods, Shor, or a global selector lie outside them. D′ changes single-modulus coverage and reaches the $1/6$ edge; it does not break Theorem A. Multiple bases with known powers merely rewrite the one-dimensional problem; genuinely different representations need separate accounting. |
+| Below16 **T1** | **PROVED in the stated model.** Fixed generator $A,B,M,K,D$, $M\mid K$; key $N\bmod K$; class set and arbitrary finite window $S$ depend only on that key. For $A\ne0$, $D/\vert A\vert \le C_1M^2$, $K\le C_2M^2$, $M,K\le P/2$; the one-term case has the normalized integrality bound stated in §2.1. Labels are charged, with per-input budgets or the two budgets $\mathbb E_{\text{all }n\text{-bit }N}\#\tau\le X$, $\mathbb E\sum\vert S_\tau\vert \le X^2$. The covered fraction of uniform $S_n$ is $\ll n^2X^{3/2}N^{-1/4}$. [§2.1](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/below16_results.md). | **PROVED by contraposition.** Constant-fraction sub-$1/6$ success must violate locality, normalized denominators, charged labels/window mass, or reliance on certified hits. Keys formed from enough CRT residues to determine $N$, cross-modulus selection, and useful giant sharing are explicit exclusions. Averaging running time only over semiprimes is not the two-moment assumption. |
+| Below16 **T2/T3** | **PROVED in their models.** T2 allows keys $(N\bmod K,\text{size box})$ with interval windows, positive degree-one weights, boxes of length between $PK$ and $2PK$, and the T1 scale bounds. T3 allows a fixed charged schedule of bounded-complexity semialgebraic regions keyed by $N\bmod M_\tau$, fixed width caps $w_\tau$, $\sum w_\tau\le X^2$, and the uniform real-fibre bound $\ll M_\tau(\sqrt{Pw_\tau}+w_\tau)$. Both retain the $1/6$ positive-fraction floor. Higher-degree Corollary F additionally assumes its real-fibre/slack hypothesis. [§§2.2–2.3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/below16_results.md). | **PROVED.** “Uses size” or “uses algebraic windows” alone is not an escape; finer size information or a window violating the proved fibre bound is. Coppersmith with supplied narrow factor information is outside the full-coverage task. The higher-degree average bound must retain its extra hypothesis. |
+| Coverage38 **Theorem U** | **PROVED in its model.** $N$-uniform groups indexed by distinct primitive weights $v$ and moduli $M$; height $h(v)=\max(\vert A\vert ,\vert B\vert )$; $J$ moduli; $\lambda=\sum\pi_g$; root/group setup charged; $X\ge T$, $X^2\ge\sum\bar w_g$. Essential **WIN**: $\bar w_g\ge cP\pi_g^2h(v)$, for the union of a group’s windows. Then $X\gg P^{2/5}\lambda^{4/5}J^{-1/5}$ and $X\gg P^{1/3}\lambda^{2/3}$. [§§1–2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/coverage38_results.md). | **PROVED.** Full and stationary-centered windows satisfy the stated WIN estimates; arbitrary efficient placements are not proved to. At $\lambda\asymp1$, no sub-$1/6$; for sub-$1/5$, coverage must occur with $\lambda<J^{1/4}$ by a power. It is **FALSE** to call WIN unconditional for all computable placements. A singleton containing the true target would violate it. |
+| Coverage38 **Proposition L** | **PROVED.** $J^2\le q/4$, forbidden sets $S_j\subset\mathbb Z/q$, $\vert S_j\vert \le q/2$, $\lambda=\sum\vert S_j\vert /q$, and $Q$ bounded additive statistics $F=\sum_j f_j(x_j)$, $\vert f_j\vert \le1$. There exist distinct $x_j\notin S_j$ with every deviation at most $2\lambda+2\sqrt{J\log(8Q)}$. [§3.2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/coverage38_results.md). | **PROVED.** Generic random-level linear-statistic estimates alone cannot force hits among unrestricted point configurations. Arithmetic identities $x_\ell=p\bar\ell\pmod q$, retained phases, or different sufficient premises can escape. This is not a lower bound on actual reciprocal-prime sums or on an algorithm. |
+| Dchworst **B1** | **PROVED conditional calculation.** If positivity is certified only from $M=\lambda+O(E)$ with usable error budget $E\gtrsim\sqrt J$, and $\lambda\asymp JH^2/Y$, the sufficient inequality $\lambda>E$ needs $H\gg Y^{1/2}J^{-1/4}$. The improved coverage design needs the smaller threshold corresponding to $c>3/8$. [§5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/dchworst_results.md). | **SPECULATIVE as a universal barrier.** No theorem says every argument incurs this error. A structured zero-hit exclusion or a phase-sensitive estimate is a possible escape. Merely saying “square-root cancellation over moduli” does not supply the necessary positivity. |
+| Dchworst **B2** | **PROVED for one-modulus pigeonholing.** Primitive weight vectors of height $\le h$ occupy only $O(h^2)$ projective residue classes modulo a prime $M$. Uniform coverage of all classes thus needs $h\gg\sqrt M$. Composite moduli can be worse for primitive vectors. [§5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/dchworst_results.md). | **PROVED.** A union over many moduli is a different existence statement. D′ uses this escape on average. The counting proof does not rule out every geometry-of-numbers argument. |
+| Dchworst **B3**, corrected by coverage38 | **PROVED conditional implication.** A coverage theorem for **all coprime integer pairs**, not just primes, specializes at $(p,q)=(x,x+1)$ to a band-prime divisor of some $bx-a$, $\vert b\vert \le2H,\vert a\vert \le H$. At the threshold this is a sparse structured union of $O(H)$ intervals of length $O(H)$, not a single interval theorem. [B3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/dchworst_results.md), [§4](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/coverage38_results.md). | **PROVED.** Such a mechanism proves the new union-coverage statement. It does not imply a named prime-gap conjecture by specialization. Linear close-pair families are Fermat/Lehman-easy and need not be covered by the divisor scheme. A “covered **or** easy” theorem escapes that specialization. |
+| Dchworst **B4** | **SPECULATIVE assessments**, not a theorem: composite moduli, Ford-type divisor distributions, continued-fraction changes of coordinates, 3D Minkowski, and input-dependent moduli. [§5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/dchworst_results.md). | **PROVED logical scope.** No universal escape requirement follows from “no gain found.” Each proposed implementation needs a cost and coverage proof. |
+| Sieve2d **Proposition 5** | **PROVED in the finite-moment information model.** Bernoulli marginals $\pi_\ell\le1/2$, comparable within a factor 2, mean $\lambda$; any fixed $J\ge2$. Exact $J$-wise independence is compatible with no-hit probability $\ge c_J\lambda^{-\lfloor J/2\rfloor}$. It follows from Peled–Yadin–Yehudayoff’s **Corollary 1.2(1)** and the odd-$J$ identity, by complementing and independent thinning. [§4.1](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/sieve2d_results.md), [PYY](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0801.0059). | **PROVED.** Fixed moments alone cannot certify a smaller universal no-hit bound. A growing-moment theorem needs its own quantitative range; arithmetic input beyond the moments also escapes. The proposition does not assert that actual uncovered prime pairs attain its adversarial distribution. |
+| Unbalanced **Proposition O′** | **PROVED in $\mathfrak L$.** Ratio band $R_0\le N/p^2\le2R_0$; arbitrary baby exponent sets of sizes $m_i$; giants test $\lfloor(\alpha p+\beta N/p+c_0)/M^2\rfloor\in g_0+S_i$ on $p\equiv r\pmod M$, with normalized rational variants and the one-term integrality refinement. $X=\sum m_i+\#G$. Covering fraction $f$ of **integer candidates** costs $\gg\min(\sqrt{fp},f^{2/3}q^{1/3})$. [§5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/unbalanced_results.md). | **PROVED.** At $q/p=N^\theta$, full coverage has floor $N^{\min((1-\theta)/4,(1+\theta)/6)}$, peaking at $1/5$ when $\theta=1/5$. This is not an average true-prime-pair floor. Pollard–Strassen supplies the first branch; D′-type searches supply the second distributionally. |
+| Track C **O1** | **PROVED.** Faithful per-prime linear readout of a fixed $R$-dimensional matrix-power/constant-coefficient recurrence state. Exact prefix-unit behavior with $R$ following zeros forces $R\ge p+1$; an exact length-$L$ interval zero set forces $R\ge L+1$. A degree-$D$ readout must be lifted to dimension $\binom{R+D}{D}$. [§3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md). | **PROVED.** The escape can be a nonlinear readout, a varying transition, a nonfaithful mixed-CRT invariant, or a different task. The bound is on dimension, not automatically time. Pollard–Strassen evaluates a product using growing degree; it does not compress this exact predicate into a tiny fixed recurrence. |
+| Track C **O2/O2′/O3** | **PROVED.** Exact interval zero set $Z_L$, $2\le L\le p-2$, $p>3$: $Q((x+u)^e)$ needs $\deg Q\ge\lceil L/2\rceil$. With $k$ translation/power layers, fibres have size at most $\prod_i\gcd(e_i,p-1)$. A nonzero polynomial with $L$ roots needs degree at least $L$. [§3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md). | **PROVED.** The multi-layer estimate becomes vacuous already at $O(\log L)$ layers with gcds 2. Degree is not circuit size. These statements do not rule out a short circuit for a degree-$L$ product. **FALSE:** “an escape must have formal degree at least $p$” or “one-layer factor-2 folding bounds all compositions.” |
+| Track C **O4** | **PROVED.** If $[1,L-1]\subset\{a:a^E=1\pmod p}$ and $L-1\ge\sqrt p+1$, then $p-1\mid E$. A proper subgroup has a least nonmember below $\sqrt p+1$: for the least $a\notin H$, use $b=\lceil p/a\rceil$ and $0<ab-p<a$. [§3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md). | **PROVED.** This identifies the $p-1$ mechanism for that power-map prefix argument. The analogue for a curve must use a point’s order and a specified embedding; no general curve-prefix theorem is proved. ECM changes the group, while class-group relations change the task. |
+| Track A **Theorem A1** | **PROVED.** $L(f)=\lambda(f(x))$, linear readout in an algebra generated by $D$ module elements. It isolates one exponent class modulo $M$ uniformly on $[0,N]$, with a nonzero allowed value at index $i\ge M$. Then $D\ge M$; generally $D\ge\min(M,i+1)$. [§3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackA_analysis.md). | **PROVED.** A monic recurrence of order $D$ for $L(x^j)$ cannot pass $M-1$ zeros and then be nonzero if $D<M$. Turning rank into time requires **dense coordinates**. A sparse, specialized circuit or nonlinear observable escapes that additional premise. Towers and CRT alone conserve the module rank. |
+| Track E information floor | **PROVED.** On the fibre $(u,c/u)\in((\mathbb Z/m)^\times)^2$, an invariant is determined by $c=N\bmod m$ iff it is constant on that fibre. Entropy statements additionally assume the stated uniform-residue distribution. [§5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackE_analysis.md). | **PROVED.** Repackaging $N\bmod m$ creates no new hint. This is no lower bound on computing a nonconstant invariant. H1 plainly computes such invariants. Coppersmith exploits a supplied **coset-shaped** hint; it does not prove that every useful hint must contain $n/4$ information bits. |
+| Eps battery | **SPECULATIVE as an asymptotic barrier.** The registered menu, sizes, controls, stratification and advantage thresholds are finite empirical evidence in [§§0–3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/eps_results.md). | **PROVED logical scope.** “No tested statistic has a detected effect of the registered magnitude” cannot exclude an untested statistic or a smaller asymptotic effect. The CF mechanisms must be compared with classical relation acquisition, not promoted to a polynomial ε oracle. |
+| Nfsfruit **Lemma H, Prop. B** | **PROVED.** For a fixed valid relation system the square-root ratio defines a linear map $\Phi:K_0\to\mathbb F_2^{\omega(N)-1}$; uniform kernel sampling succeeds with probability $1-2^{-\operatorname{rank}\Phi}$. For globally uniform $r$ and $\lambda=r^2\bmod N$, the probability of a ratio representative $c/d$, $0<\vert c\vert ,\vert d\vert \le H$, is at most $4H^2/(\varphi(N)/2^{\omega(N)})$. [§§3, 5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nfsfruit_results.md). | **PROVED.** More parity dependencies do not prove $\Phi\ne0$. Globally uniform hidden roots rarely admit tiny ratio representatives, by counting those ratios. Fibre-wise uniformity or another root source escapes the ratio count. Dixon has the required hidden-root symmetry; ordinary NFS relation sampling does not automatically inherit it. |
+| LemmaM barriers | **PROVED in the specified architectures.** Even-cardinality two-linear-form sieves with the stated no-known-root setup are trivial if all factor-base primes have Jacobi $+1$. Uniform-class relation cost $\max(b+1/(4b),2b)\ge1$. A one-level descent priced using $L_{B_1}[2/3]$, with $B_1=L_N[\beta_1]$, requires $\alpha\ge2\beta_1/3$ and $\alpha\ge1-\beta_1$, hence $\alpha\ge2/5$. [§§2, 5–6](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/lemmaM_results.md). | **PROVED.** QS and hidden-root constructions escape the first scope; LP already supplies rigorous $L[1/2,1]$. The $2/5$ statement prices a particular splitting routine; it is not a lower bound against a new smoothness test or multilevel descent. |
+| HypD **B1–B7 / Prop. G** | **PROVED accounting/geometric restrictions; other steps remain SPECULATIVE.** An all-moduli logarithmic error saving need not control an $L^{-c}$-density subfamily. For two primitive special-$q$ vectors the joint-value lattice has extra index $D\ge\ell$; ignoring that congruence support loses the indicated factor. Growing-$k$ product laws need stronger precision than fixed-$k$ laws. Fixed-$k$ rigorous shape-test costs and the one-level $2/5$ accounting cannot be silently replaced by $L[1/3]$. [§§3, 4, 6](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/hypD_results.md). | **PROVED.** A viable proof must respect the sparse subfamily, moving residues, joint lattice support, and actual smoothness-test price. Rank completion and fruitfulness are different claims. H3 itself does not imply any particular one of the proposed FM/PC/RBDH′/RK hypotheses. |
+
+**PROVED (proof mechanisms behind the main counting rows).** For degree-one full-cover targets, the lock normalization and curvature bound give at most $C\sqrt{P W_\tau}+C$ covered candidates. Thus
+
+$$
+\#\text{covered}\le C\sqrt P\sum_\tau\sqrt{W_\tau}+CX
+\le C\sqrt P\,X^{3/2}+CX.
+$$
+
+Covering $\asymp P$ candidates forces $X\gg P^{1/3}=N^{1/6}$. Higher-degree support-$k$, degree-$e$ targets have the source bound $CP^{1-e/k}W^{1/k}+C$; Hölder and $k\le2e$, $e\ge2$, give the $N^{1/5}$ branch. T1’s different, true-pair count is proved in §8 rather than replacing actual primes by all candidates.
+
+**PROVED (U’s algebra).** At one modulus with $t_M$ distinct weights, the height count gives $\sum_v1/h(v)\ll\sqrt{t_M}$. Cauchy gives $\sum_v\pi_v^2h(v)\gg u_M^2/\sqrt{t_M}$, $u_M=\sum_v\pi_v$. Hölder over moduli yields $\sum_Mu_M^2/\sqrt{t_M}\ge\lambda^2/\sqrt{TJ}$. Insert WIN and $T\le X$, $\sum\bar w\le X^2$; then $X^{5/2}\gg P\lambda^2/\sqrt J$. Using $J\le T\le X$ gives the second bound. The inequalities do not manufacture WIN.
+
+**PROVED (L’s scope).** Independently draw each $x_j$ uniformly outside $S_j$. Avoiding the forbidden set shifts the mean of a bounded statistic by at most $2|S_j|/q$. Hoeffding plus a union bound controls all $Q$ statistics with positive probability at the stated scale; the condition $J^2\le q/4$ leaves positive probability of distinctness as well. These fake zero-hit configurations need not satisfy the actual reciprocal-prime identities. That last distinction is the available escape.
+
+### 7.2 Ward–Maestro floors
+
+**KNOWN (restricted models).** [Ward–Maestro, *Floors for deterministic integer factoring*](https://igent.ai/research/floors-not-bounds/factoring_report.pdf), Sept. 2026:
+
+| Scope | Assumptions ⇒ floor; exact locator |
+|---|---|
+| **KNOWN: cells** | Positive integer forms $aq+bp$; real-range coverage; tested integer sets $T_i$, $W_i=\vert T_i\vert $. Materialized $T\ge m+\sum_i\lceil W_i/m\rceil$; free $T\ge m+\sum_iW_i/m$. Floors $N^{1/5},N^{1/6}$: Definition 2.1, Theorems 3.4/3.6. |
+| **KNOWN: thinning/robustness** | Enumerated residue hypotheses, modulus $q\le N^{1/20}$: $N^{1/5}(\varphi(q)/q)^{3/5}$, Theorem 2.8. Fixed-degree forms: Theorem 9.4; adaptive cell-membership coverage: Proposition 9.5. |
+| **KNOWN: explicit differences** | Explicit $B-G$ cover; start-statistic envelope $D_{\max}\le r^\eta$: $N^{(1-\eta)/(5-4\eta)}$, Theorem 4.4. Polynomial-size divisibility objects: $N^{1/4}$, Proposition 4.5. |
+| **KNOWN: pieces/localization** | Separate pieces priced $\ge\sqrt{W_j}$, $H=\max(1,\max_j\sum_{i\in I_j:\,a_i\le b_i\le\lceil Ca_i\rceil}1/a_i)$: $N^{1/4}/\sqrt H$, Theorem 10.1. M1, $N^{1/4}\le L\le\sqrt N$: $\min(LN^{-1/4}/\operatorname{polylog}N,L^{3/5}N^{-1/10})$, Theorem 13.2; position/depth refinement, Theorem 13.15. |
+| **KNOWN / SPECULATIVE: NFS** | Uniform root, $H<\min(p,q)$: probability $\le d^2(2H+1)^{d+1}/N$, Proposition 38.1. Coprime common-root resultant floor: Proposition 40.1. Equidistributed-root **heuristic**: $\min(\Vertf\Vert_\infty m)=N^{(2d-1)/(d^2+d-1)+o(1)}$; GNFS-constant optimum, Propositions 39.1/42.1. |
+| **KNOWN: other parts** | Fixed-list ECM exposure criterion: Proposition 32.4, not a universal lower bound. Common-($p-1,q-1$) algorithms: Proposition 28.2; positive-density $p-1$ success: Proposition 35.3. |
+
+**PROVED (independent resultant inequality).** If coprime integer polynomials $f,g$ of degrees $d,e$ share a root modulo $N$, their nonzero resultant is divisible by $N$: the Sylvester resultant identity specializes to zero at that root. Hadamard’s determinant bound gives
+
+$$
+N\le|\operatorname{Res}(f,g)|
+\le (d+1)^{e/2}(e+1)^{d/2}\|f\|_\infty^e\|g\|_\infty^d.
+$$
+
+This elementary inequality survives fast factoring. It constrains these explicit polynomial pairs, not all ways of finding a factor. A bound on coefficients alone does not prohibit a different relation architecture or improve an analytic smoothness estimate.
+
+**PROVED (how to read this together with the repository).** The supplied notes’ full-cover and local-pair models are different quantifiers. D′ can beat a materialized single-family floor while obeying a broader $1/6$ floor. Supplied factor information changes the search problem, as the Coppersmith example below illustrates. A faster algorithm that never constructs the tested cell families has no obligation to violate their geometric lemmas.
+
+### 7.3 Which known algorithm families have which escapes?
+
+The labels in the running-time column separate established algorithms from unproved analyses. “Outside” means the model’s definition does not contain that method, not that a theorem has been disproved.
+
+| Family | Coverage/locality and information | Relations/order information | Determinism and bound |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Pollard–Strassen / BGS products | **PROVED.** Divisibility by interval products, not a degree-one true-factor selector. Dense degree grows; product trees give a square-root saving in interval length. | **PROVED.** Direct nonunit detection; no need for smooth $p-1$. | **KNOWN.** Deterministic $N^{1/4+o(1)}$ general bound; see Bostan–Gaudry–Schost, SIAM J. Comput. 36 (2007), **Theorem 5** ([primary paper](https://mathexp.eu/bostan/publications/BoGaSc07.pdf)) for the evaluation ingredient, as used in [Track C §1](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md). It supplies no sub-$1/6$ general algorithm. |
+| Harvey / Harvey–Hittmeir | **KNOWN.** Explicit degree-one collision targets and arithmetic thinning. Harvey, Math. Comp. 90 (2021), **Theorem 1.1**; HH, *A log-log speedup for exponent one-fifth deterministic integer factorisation*, Math. Comp. 91 (2022), **Theorem 1.1**. | **PROVED.** Collision identity uses $p-1$, but not only a fixed list of smooth-order annihilators. | **KNOWN.** Worst-case exponent $1/5$; the refinement improves logarithms. [Harvey](https://doi.org/10.1090/mcom/3658). |
+| D′ / AV | **PROVED in supplied notes.** Many-modulus selective coverage, within T1 after the stated budget domination. | **PROVED.** Shared collision machinery; average coverage and extraction, not merely a fixed smooth-order list. | **PROVED in supplied notes.** Deterministic $N^{1/6}\operatorname{polylog}N$ mean on uniform $S_n$, with $N^{1/5}$ fallback; [AV](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/avgtime_results.md), [D′](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/dcltheorem_results.md). It attains, rather than escapes, the local floor. |
+| Pollard $p-1$, ECM | **PROVED.** Success is group-order/point-order divisibility, outside cell coverage. ECM varies curves. | **PROVED.** One-sided annihilation must separate the two prime components. Fixed-list certificates are covered by §8; input-dependent choices need not be. | **SPECULATIVE at the usual subexponential ECM bound.** Its familiar expected $L_p[1/2,\sqrt2]$ analysis needs the relevant smooth-order supply. A positive-density $p-1$ theorem is not an almost-all or worst-case factorizer. |
+| QS / NFS | **PROVED.** Relation collection and global linear algebra, outside the charged sumset-cover definition. | **PROVED.** Build a square congruence from dependencies; fruitfulness is an additional issue. | **SPECULATIVE.** The standard QS $L[1/2]$, GNFS $L[1/3,(64/9)^{1/3}]$, and straightforward deterministic enumerations with those costs rely on relation-supply/fruitfulness assumptions. A heuristic deterministic candidate family can have the desired structural signature; it is not a proved deterministic bound. |
+| Schnorr–Lenstra / Seysen / LP | **KNOWN.** Quadratic-class-group relation methods are outside local Lehman coverage. Seysen, Math. Comp. 48 (1987), **Proposition 8.6 / Theorem 8.7**, gives the subgroup-based extraction context; LP’s **§1 main theorem** gives the unconditional randomized $L[1/2,1]$ bound. | **PROVED.** Smooth ideals, relations, and ambiguous forms; not an identity $C_N^{h(p,q)}=D_N$ with a known short true-factor exponent. | **KNOWN / SPECULATIVE.** LP’s randomized bound is rigorous; the earlier analyses have their own GRH or smoothness assumptions. Deterministically enumerating the random choices does not preserve the expectation bound. |
+| Coppersmith | **KNOWN.** Given roughly $\frac14\log_2N$ high or low bits of a factor, polynomial-time lattice decoding factors $N=pq$: Coppersmith, J. Cryptology 10 (1997), **Theorems 4–5**. These are outside full-range cell coverage. [Article](https://doi.org/10.1007/s001459900030). | **PROVED.** Uses arithmetic smallness and lattices, not only group orders. | **PROVED conditional use.** Polynomial decoding with a sufficiently precise supplied hint is not a polynomial algorithm that obtains the hint. |
+| Shor | **KNOWN.** Quantum order finding and measurement, outside classical collision accounting and classical generic-query bounds. Shor, SIAM J. Comput. 26 (1997), **§§4–5, order-finding/factoring algorithms**. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9508027). | **PROVED.** Supplies an exact order via a quantum procedure, then uses the classical order-to-factor reduction. | **KNOWN.** Randomized quantum polynomial time; not a classical deterministic escape. |
+
+**PROVED (intersection of necessary escapes).** For a classical deterministic factorizer with worst-case cost $N^{1/6-\delta}$, certified local T1/T2/T3 coverage cannot remain its sole success certificate. If it still uses cell coverage, it must also leave the appropriate full-cover/cost model; if it uses the O1/A1 sketches, it must leave their linear/dense-representation premises. These are **conditional membership tests**, not a demand that every algorithm break every row. A relation algorithm can avoid the entire cell family. A group-only algorithm can avoid cell locality yet still face an unresolved deterministic order-supply or separation problem.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (which family is closest).** No family in this table has a **proved classical deterministic** worst-case or balanced-almost-all $N^{1/6-\delta}$ guarantee. QS/NFS are the clearest structurally compatible candidates; LP shows that a relation/class-group escape can already be made rigorous with randomness. Shor meets a stronger speed goal by changing the computational model. This is an audit conclusion about the listed families, not a proof that an unlisted family cannot work.
+
+## 8. A proved combined barrier, and its limits
+
+### 8.1 A quantitative extension of the T1 locality calculation
+
+Define the key enlargement parameter
+
+$$
+\kappa=\max_\iota\max(1,K_\iota/M_\iota^2).
+$$
+
+**PROVED (key-enlargement lemma).** Retain T1’s fixed generators, $M\mid K$, $M,K\le P/2$, normalized $D/|A|\ll M^2$, and its charged budgets; drop only the bound $K\ll M^2$. The number of covered prime pairs in any fixed balanced subbox is at most
+
+$$
+C P^{3/2}\sqrt\kappa\,X^{3/2}.
+\tag{8.1}
+$$
+
+Consequently the covered fraction in a prime-pair box of cardinality $\gg P^2/n^2$ is at most
+
+$$
+C n^2\sqrt\kappa\,X^{3/2}N^{-1/4}.
+\tag{8.2}
+$$
+
+Proof: fix the class prime $x$ and key $c=N\bmod K$. Its possible partner integers form one progression modulo $K$. Each permitted target value confines that partner to an interval of length $D/|A|$; the entire partner range has length $O(P)$. If the window has $s$ values, the count is at most
+
+$$
+\min\!\left(s\left(\frac{D}{|A|K}+1\right),\frac{CP}{K}\right)
+\le \frac{C\sqrt{Ps(D/|A|+K)}}K.
+$$
+
+There are $O(P/M)$ class-variable integers per allowed residue. Summing by keys and comparing each key with its frequency among all $n$-bit inputs gives
+
+$$
+\#\text{pairs for a generator}
+\le C\frac{P^{3/2}}M\sqrt{D/|A|+K}\,
+\mathbb E_{\rm all\ inputs}(|R|\sqrt s)
+\le CP^{3/2}\sqrt\kappa\,\mathbb E(|R|\sqrt s).
+$$
+
+Sum over generators, counting each allowed class as a target, and apply Cauchy–Schwarz:
+
+$$
+\mathbb E\sum_\tau\sqrt{|S_\tau|}
+\le\sqrt{(\mathbb E\#\tau)(\mathbb E\sum|S_\tau|)}\le X^{3/2}.
+$$
+
+For a one-term target, swap the two prime variables and transform its class by $r\mapsto cr^{-1}\pmod M$; it becomes the nonzero-partner-coefficient case, with no larger class set. This proves (8.1), and division gives (8.2). The source of the expectation is **all $n$-bit integers**, just as in T1; an average over semiprimes alone cannot be substituted.
+
+**PROVED (a necessary size for this escape).** If this enlarged-key model covers a constant fraction at cost $N^{1/6-\delta}$, then
+
+$$
+\kappa\ge N^{3\delta}/\operatorname{polylog}N.
+\tag{8.3}
+$$
+
+Substitute into (8.2) and solve. Merely replacing a local key by another key of comparable size does not escape the exponent.
+
+### 8.2 Local coverage plus fixed group-order exposure
+
+The following is one precise interpretation of “local in sense X and uses only group-order information in sense Y.” It is deliberately narrower than arbitrary access to exact group orders.
+
+**Model X.** Certified local hits are those of the key-enlarged T1 model above, with budget $X$. Any continuation after unsuccessful exposure tests must still satisfy that model; failures cannot secretly choose unrestricted global weights.
+
+**Model Y.** The extra mechanism uses a public, input-independent list of integer vanishing certificates $V_1,\ldots,V_t$, represented in any way whose evaluation costs are charged. It can split only by the gcds of these values with $N$. Its prime signature is
+
+$$
+\sigma(p)=(1_{p\mid V_1},\ldots,1_{p\mid V_t}).
+$$
+
+More generally any fixed-list order procedure is allowed if its successful splitting **requires different signatures** for the two prime components. Let $R$ be the number of distinct signatures on the relevant primes. The certificates may be huge, evaluated modulo $N$; their *prime dependence* must be fixed independently of $N=pq$. Fixed-base $p-1$ annihilators and fixed-curve, fixed-bound ECM atoms are examples after denominators have been included as certificates.
+
+**PROVED (combined local/exposure theorem).** Choose a prime interval $I\subset[cP,CP]$ of fixed relative length, with $\Pi\gg P/n$ primes, so that all products of distinct primes in $I$ lie in one $n$-bit balanced range. On uniform unordered pairs from this interval, an X+Y algorithm failing on at most a fraction ε satisfies
+
+$$
+C n^2\sqrt\kappa\,X^{3/2}N^{-1/4}
+\ge \frac1R-\varepsilon-O(1/\Pi).
+\tag{8.4}
+$$
+
+In particular, if it succeeds on **every** pair, $R=o(\Pi)$, then
+
+$$
+X\gg
+\frac{N^{1/6}}{\kappa^{1/3}R^{2/3}n^{4/3}}.
+\tag{8.5}
+$$
+
+Proof: partition the $\Pi$ primes by signature, with class sizes $a_1,\ldots,a_R$. The equal-signature pair fraction is
+
+$$
+\frac{\sum_j a_j(a_j-1)}{\Pi(\Pi-1)}
+\ge\frac{\Pi/R-1}{\Pi-1}
+\ge\frac1R-O(1/\Pi),
+$$
+
+using $\sum a_j^2\ge\Pi^2/R$. On each such pair, every certificate gcd is 1 or $N$, so Model Y cannot split. Apart from the allowed failures, Model X must cover these pairs. Apply (8.2). Rearranging proves (8.5). The prime-count assumption is the usual fixed-proportion interval consequence of PNT; it can also simply be retained as an explicit cardinality hypothesis of the finite theorem. No distributional assertion about smooth group orders is used.
+
+**PROVED (escape inequality).** For worst-case $X=N^{1/6-\delta}$, the theorem requires
+
+$$
+\kappa R^2\ge N^{3\delta}/\operatorname{polylog}N.
+\tag{8.6}
+$$
+
+For the original locality bound $\kappa=O(1)$, this means $R\ge N^{3\delta/2}/\operatorname{polylog}N$. Since binary tests have $R\le2^t$, at least $(3\delta/2)\log_2N-O(\log\log N)$ binary tests are necessary; this is a cardinality requirement, not an independence assertion. For polynomial $X$ and bounded κ, the corresponding condition is $R\ge N^{1/4}/\operatorname{polylog}N$.
+
+**PROVED (almost-all qualification).** If $\kappa,R=N^{o(1)}$ and $\varepsilon=o(1/R)$, the same theorem gives the $N^{1/6-o(1)}$ floor. An unspecified ε $=o(1)$ is insufficient when $R\to\infty$: all remaining equal-signature pairs might fit inside that exceptional set. With constant $R$, ε $=o(1)$ suffices. This distinction prevents a false upgrade from worst case to almost all.
+
+**PROVED (limits).** This theorem does not outlaw a polynomial-time algorithm: $t=\Theta(n)$ can already allow exponentially many signatures. It also does not cover $N$-dependent exponents or curves, full exact order outputs, hidden cross-correlations between the local and order stages, useful accidental collisions, or a relation algorithm. Extending it to those cases requires additional mathematics. It is a double-counting theorem, not an unrestricted computational lower bound.
+
+**FALSE (a broader formulation).** “Locality plus any use of group orders implies an $N^{1/6}$ lower bound.” Without a precise restriction on what the order information is allowed to reveal, the statement includes an exact-order oracle, which factors by §3.2, and includes smooth-order methods lying outside T1. The correct combined statement is (8.4), with its explicit premises.
+
+## 9. Q3(i–ii): independent blockers and useful reverse reductions
+
+### 9.1 Which consequences deserve the word blocker?
+
+| Hypothesis or mechanism | Established consequence | Strength as a blocker |
+|---|---|---|
+| H1 | **PROVED.** Deterministic polynomial μ, squarefreeness, σ, φ, radicals, maximal orders, residuosity decision, and the listed composite-modulus counts. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** These settle recognized algorithmic problems but do not conflict with their mathematical definitions or existing lower bounds. They are checkpoints for a candidate, not independent impossibility evidence. |
+| H2 | **PROVED.** Randomized versions of the above and polynomial randomized order finding. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** No analytic conjecture or complexity collapse beyond those reductions was identified. It refutes the relevant factoring-hardness assumptions. |
+| Rigorous H3 | **PROVED.** A new proved subexponential factoring upper bound. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** That does not require proving GNFS smoothness or NFS fruitfulness unless the proposed algorithm uses them. For a GNFS-like proof, those are genuine mechanism-dependent open inputs. |
+| Worst-case H4 via Theorem R | **PROVED in repository framework.** The proposed coverage certificate must meet U’s low-$\lambda$ threshold; its all-coprime-pair version implies the sparse $U(x,H)$ statement of B3. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** This is a sharper, noncircular obstacle for that mechanism. The sparse union statement is not a known prime-gap conjecture and need not follow from an unrelated H4 algorithm. |
+| Sub-$1/6$ H4 within X+Y | **PROVED.** The inequalities (8.3)/(8.6) force quantified key enlargement or many prime signatures, or departure from X+Y. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** Cheap and decisive for a precisely specified candidate. It does not rule out relation methods or unrestricted bit computation. |
+| H5 squarefreeness | **KNOWN, BL Theorem 6.12.** Polynomial squarefreeness gives polynomial maximal-order recognition. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** A separate useful open algorithmic problem; not a verified reduction to general factoring. |
+| H5 full uniform totient residues | **PROVED, §9.2.** CRT gives exact φ, and the semiprime formula factors. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** A strong warning that “small outputs” can collectively encode the entire search problem. |
+| A uniformly short exact-factorial circuit mechanism | **PROVED, §5.1.** It solves modular factorial thresholds and factoring; published arithmetic-complexity implications involve still stronger nonuniform hypotheses. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** This is a useful additional obligation of the mechanism. Fast factoring alone does not force it. |
+
+**PROVED (no automatic NP collapse).** Factoring a positive binary integer is a total search problem with an efficiently checked, unique sorted prime-power answer. Its threshold decision version also has certificates for the “no small factor” case: provide the complete prime factorization and primality certificates. A polynomial-time factoring algorithm would solve this problem; it does not, without an NP-hardness reduction, solve all of NP. None of the reductions in this report supplies such a reduction. The same reasoning applies to a claim that H1 proves $P=BPP$: solving one randomized problem does not derandomize every other one.
+
+### 9.2 Small totient residues: a complete reduction and a weak bit
+
+**PROVED (uniform small-modulus CRT reduction).** Suppose an oracle, for a fixed input $N$, returns $\varphi(N)\bmod m$ for every requested prime modulus $m\le Cn$, with fixed sufficiently large $C$. Take distinct such primes until their product $M>N$. There are $O(n/\log n)$ of them by the prime number theorem, their enumeration is polynomial time, and CRT reconstructs the unique integer $0\le\varphi(N)<N$. For $N=pq$, §3.2 then factors deterministically. On arbitrary $N$, the multiple-of-λ reduction gives randomized polynomial-time factoring. The bit cost of the CRT stage is polynomial in $n$.
+
+**PROVED (elementary alternative without prime-density estimates).** Query moduli $2,3,\ldots,2n+2$, or just their maximal prime powers. Their lcm exceeds $N$: $\operatorname{lcm}(1,\ldots,k)\ge2^{k-1}/k$ follows because each binomial coefficient $\binom{k-1}{j}$ divides that lcm, and the largest coefficient is at least $2^{k-1}/k$. With $k=2n+2$ this exceeds $2^n$ for all sufficiently large $n$; finitely many smaller inputs are handled directly. Consistent residue reconstruction modulo the lcm therefore also recovers φ using only $O(n)$ moduli of size $O(n)$. To justify the divisibility assertion, for each prime $p$, $v_p\binom{k-1}{j}$ counts carries in base $p$ and is at most $\lfloor\log_p k\rfloor$, the exponent available in the lcm. Alternatively this bound follows immediately by subtracting the three factorial-valuation sums.
+
+**PROVED (balanced sum reconstruction).** For $p<q\le2p$, $2\sqrt N<s=p+q\le(3/\sqrt2)\sqrt N$. Thus $s\bmod M$ with $M>(3/\sqrt2-2)\sqrt N+O(1)$ leaves at most one possible integer $s$ in the public interval. Knowing φ modulo such an $M$ supplies $s\equiv N+1-\varphi(N)\pmod M$, and the discriminant factors $N$. Fewer residues may feed a lattice decoder instead, but the shape and ambiguity must be charged.
+
+**PROVED (one fixed bit is different).** For distinct odd $p,q$ with $N\equiv1\pmod4$,
+
+$$
+\varphi(N)\equiv
+\begin{cases}
+0\pmod8,&p\equiv q\equiv1\pmod4,\\
+4\pmod8,&p\equiv q\equiv3\pmod4.
+\end{cases}
+$$
+
+Hence φ modulo 8 is exactly the ε/two-square distinction on this promise. The calculation uses $(p-1)(q-1)$; φ modulo 2 or 4 supplies no such distinction on this promise. This equivalence of **bits** does not turn one modulo-8 oracle into the uniform CRT oracle.
+
+**PROVED (2-adic hint geometry).** If $v=v_2(q-p)$, solutions to $x^2-sx+N=0\pmod{2^k}$ are determined by $v_2(x-p)+v_2(x-q)\ge k$. For $k>2v$, they are two cosets modulo $2^{k-v}$; for $k\le2v$, one coset modulo $2^{\lceil k/2\rceil}$. For $v_2(x-p)<v$, both valuations are equal; above $v$, the other valuation is exactly $v$, proving the alternatives. This explains the $v$-bit loss in [Track E, Proposition E2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackE_analysis.md). A sufficient known-bits decoder threshold is not an information-theoretic necessity theorem.
+
+### 9.3 μ, squarefreeness, ω, and the divisor function
+
+**PROVED (the easy decision arrows).** A full μ oracle decides squarefreeness by $\mu(N)\ne0$. A radical oracle does so by $\operatorname{rad}(N)=N$. Ordinary Jacobi symbols require no factoring oracle: strip factors of 2 using $(2/b)=(-1)^{(b^2-1)/8}$, swap odd arguments using quadratic reciprocity, and reduce the numerator modulo the denominator. Euclidean descent uses $O(\log N)$ steps on $O(\log N)$-bit integers. This supplies an unconditional deterministic polynomial-time algorithm; an unknown-factor Legendre bit is a different interface.
+
+**PROVED (why the semiprime promise matters).** On distinct-prime semiprimes, $\omega(N)=2$, $\Omega(N)=2$, $d(N)=4$, $\mu(N)=1$, squarefreeness is true, and $\operatorname{rad}(N)=N$. These functions on **that promise alone** reveal no factor. An all-integer oracle can query transformed inputs; proving a useful transformation is the missing reduction, not a consequence of the constant promise values.
+
+**PROVED (one divisor-count query computes μ).** Choose a prime $q>1+\log_2N$ of size $O(\log N)$, deterministically by small-number enumeration. Ask for
+
+$$
+D=d(N^{q-1})=\prod_{p^a\parallel N}(1+(q-1)a).
+$$
+
+Every exponent $a<q$. A factor in this product is divisible by $q$ iff $a=1$, in which case it equals $q$. If any $a>1$, its factor is $>1$ and coprime to $q$. Therefore $D$ is a pure power $q^r$ iff $N$ is squarefree; then μ $=(-1)^r$, otherwise μ $=0$. All inputs and outputs have polynomial bit length. This proves the direction associated with Shallit–Shamir, *Number-theoretic functions which are equivalent to number of divisors*, IPL 20 (1985), pp. 151–153, without needing an unverified theorem number. [Original abstract](https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(85)90084-5).
+
+**PROVED (exponent histogram from divisor counts).** Put $r\le n$ for the number of prime factors and $Q(t)=d(N^t)=\prod_i(1+e_it)$, a degree-$r$ polynomial. Values at $t=0,\ldots,n$ recover $Q$ by exact rational interpolation. For each $a=1,\ldots,n$, repeatedly divide by $1+at$; the multiplicity recovered is the number of primes of exponent $a$. This computes any efficiently postprocessed function of the exponent multiset. It identifies no prime labels. The queried integers have up to $O(n^2)$ bits, so this is a polynomial-bit reduction, not a preservation of an H4 numerical exponent.
+
+**PROVED (radical from an exact φ oracle).** Set $m_0=N$, and recursively
+
+$$
+g_i=\gcd(m_i,\varphi(m_i)),\qquad r_i=m_i/g_i,\qquad m_{i+1}=g_i.
+$$
+
+Since $v_p(\varphi(m_i))\ge v_p(m_i)-1$, each $r_i$ is squarefree. For $m_i>1$, $g_i<m_i$, so there are at most $\log_2N$ iterations. The product of the $r_i$ is $N$, and consequently $\operatorname{rad}(N)=\operatorname{lcm}_i r_i$. This proves a deterministic polynomial-time **Turing** reduction radical ≤ φ, and hence squarefreeness ≤ φ. It is the direction relevant to Landau’s *Some remarks on computing the square parts of integers*, Inf. Comput. 78 (1988), and Woll’s *Reductions among number theoretic problems*, Inf. Comput. 72 (1987); no reverse factoring reduction from a squarefreeness bit is being claimed. [Landau](https://doi.org/10.1016/0890-5401(88)90028-4), [Woll](https://doi.org/10.1016/0890-5401(87)90030-7).
+
+**PROVED (squarefree decomposition from radicals).** Repeatedly divide a remaining integer by its radical. A prime occurring with exponent $e$ appears in exactly the first $e$ radical layers. Ratios of successive layers give the products of primes with each exact exponent. Thus full radical construction yields squarefree decomposition in polynomially many calls, but a decision-only squarefreeness oracle does not supply these layers by this proof.
+
+**PROVED (a broad useless family of special shapes).** For squarefree $N=pq$, $\gcd(N,k)=1$, multiplicativity gives $\mu(Nk)=\mu(k)$, and (Nk) is squarefree iff $k$ is squarefree. For $k>1$, $\mu(Nk^2)=0$. Likewise $\omega(Nk)=2+\omega(k)$. Consequently querying these coprime multiplicative masks, whose own factorization is known, cannot extract a factor of $N$. If a mask is not coprime, its gcd with $N$ already supplies a factor. This is a concrete kill criterion for one family of μ/squarefreeness-oracle proposals.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (remaining reverse reductions).** This audit does not establish general polynomial reductions factoring ≤ μ, factoring ≤ squarefreeness decision, factoring ≤ ω, factoring ≤ two-square decision, or factoring ≤ quadratic-residuosity decision in the ordinary bit model. Neither the σ theorem nor the divisor-count-to-μ construction supplies these arrows. “Not established” is not an oracle-separation theorem or a proof of impossibility.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (historical citation audit).** I did not verify an exact Woll theorem asserting a relativized separation involving μ or squarefreeness; the checked reduction is consistent with the φ direction proved above. Adleman–McCurley’s *Open problems in number theoretic complexity, II*, ANTS 1994, is a historical problem list, not a theorem closing these gaps; no unverified problem number is used here. [Original chapter](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-58691-1_70). These are explicit limits of this literature audit.
+
+**KNOWN (a useful reverse point-counting theorem).** Dąbrowski–Pomykała–Shparlinski, *On oracle factoring of integers*, J. Complexity 76 (2023), 101741, **Definition 1.1 / Theorem 1.2**, use the counts $E(m,b)=\prod_{p\mid m}\#E_b(\mathbb F_p)$ for $E_b:y^2=x(x^2-b)$. Their oracle returns polynomially bounded positive multiples for squarefree $m$ and $\gcd(b,m)=1$, and signals nonsquarefreeness otherwise. For $2<\gamma<2+(\sqrt{257}-15)/16$, $0<d<1/4$, and $b\le(\log Z)^\gamma$, a deterministic algorithm finds a factor for all but $O(Z/(\log Z)^d)$ integers $m\le Z$, using $O((\log Z)^\gamma)$ queries and time
+
+$$
+O((\log Z)^{\rho(\gamma)+o(1)}),\qquad
+\rho(\gamma)=\max\{\gamma+2,(9\gamma-17)/(8(\gamma-2))\}.
+$$
+
+The oracle bound is $Z^{O(1)}$; polynomial oracle time is an assumption. **Theorem 1.4** gives a one-query product-count variant with a weaker exceptional set. [Primary paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1912.00345).
+
+**PROVED (distributional limit of that theorem).** An exceptional set of size $Z/(\log Z)^d$, $d<1/4$, is large enough to contain every balanced semiprime in a fixed dyadic prime box, whose size is $\asymp Z/(\log Z)^2$. Therefore this theorem alone does not imply an almost-all **balanced-semiprime** reduction. It nevertheless supplies a precise family of point-count outputs for reverse-reduction experiments.
+
+### 9.4 A harmless consequence that is directly usable: testing fruitfulness
+
+**PROVED (linear-algebra decision-to-search).** Let $A\in\mathbb F_2^{d\times r}$ encode relation parities, and let $c\in\mathbb F_2^r$ encode a valid computable fruitfulness functional on its kernel. There exists a dependency $v$ with $Av=0$, $c\cdot v=1$, iff
+
+$$
+c\notin\operatorname{rowspan}(A),
+\qquad
+\operatorname{rank}\!\begin{bmatrix}A\\c\end{bmatrix}=\operatorname{rank}(A)+1.
+\tag{9.1}
+$$
+
+Proof: $(\ker A)^\perp=\operatorname{rowspan}(A)$. Gaussian elimination either expresses $c$ in that rowspace or constructs the indicated $v$ by solving the augmented system. This is a polynomial-time **deterministic** search once a suitable relation system is present. On the Blum/QS and degree-one setups where the repository proves the computable Jacobi row, it is directly operational. It does not prove that relation generation ever produces the rank increase.
+
+**PROVED (a harmless symmetry, with its exact premise).** Suppose a relation transcript and dependency selection depend on a sampled root $u$ only through $u^2\bmod N$. Condition on that transcript and all other randomness. Suppose the chosen dependency uses this root to an odd power, so one extracted square root is (uW) for a fixed unit $W$, while the other root is fixed by the transcript. The first root is uniform in the common CRT fibre, and the second is fixed; success is $1-2^{1-\omega(N)}$ by §3.3. This is the Dixon-fibre mechanism in [LemmaM, Theorem DD](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/lemmaM_results.md). Revealing a sign-sensitive predicate or choosing the dependency from hidden sign data destroys the premise. Global uniformity of the square is unnecessary.
+
+## 10. Q3(iii): ranked research directions
+
+The ranking favors a short decisive calculation or lemma over a broad new factoring program. Feasibility estimates are **SPECULATIVE** judgments, not numerical probabilities.
+
+### Rank 1 — audit the key size of a genuinely input-dependent selector
+
+**SPECULATIVE (proposal; high feasibility for a decisive audit, low to moderate for a positive escape).** Specify one selector that inspects more of $N$ than $N\bmod O(M^2)$, while retaining a computable collision certificate. Measure its key size $K$, classes, windows, and evaluation cost on the same input distribution. Start with selectors based on several cross-modulus residues or a short continued-fraction prefix. The object to test is the conditional placement of useful windows, rather than another unconditional correlation statistic.
+
+**PROVED (first lemma already available).** The key-enlargement lemma (8.1) says that gaining a fixed exponent $\delta$ through this particular escape requires $\kappa\ge N^{3\delta}/\operatorname{polylog}N$. Thus a proposed selector with $K=M^2N^{o(1)}$ cannot beat the exponent in this model. For a concrete large-key selector, the next lemma to prove is that its certified coverage gain survives the charged cost of constructing the key-dependent windows.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (kill criterion).** Kill the proposal if its key can be compressed back into $O(M^2)N^{o(1)}$ without changing the targets, if it needs factor information to place the windows, or if its extra construction cost cancels the claimed exponent saving. A one-page key/cost certificate should precede numerical success rates.
+
+### Rank 2 — classify partial-information reductions on promise-preserving shapes
+
+**SPECULATIVE (proposal; high feasibility for exclusions, low for a general reduction).** Treat ε, φ modulo 8, arbitrary QR decision, and uniform small-modulus φ as distinct oracles. Search for a polynomial-bit transformation $N\mapsto N_j$ that preserves the two-distinct-prime, $1\bmod4$ promise while turning an ε answer into a useful bit about the original factors. Separately test additive shapes for μ/squarefreeness; coprime multiplicative masks are already excluded by §9.3.
+
+**PROVED (first lemma and baseline).** On the promised semiprimes, ε is equivalent to φ modulo 8 by §9.2. Uniform φ residues reconstruct full φ. A claimed interpolation between these endpoints must therefore state exactly which moduli or transformed inputs it generates. One valid next lemma would be a reduction of general Jacobi-$+1$ residuosity to ε on explicitly constructed semiprimes, with the promise and query bit lengths proved. This is a research target, not a supplied reduction.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (kill criterion).** Reject transformations whose outputs require finding fresh factors, whose promise verification is as hard as the target, or whose answers reduce algebraically to known-mask data. Record oracle-query growth: replacing an $n$-bit query by an $n^2$-bit one preserves polynomial-bit complexity but destroys a claimed preservation of an H4 numerical exponent.
+
+### Rank 3 — force a relation-space rank increase with a certified sampling symmetry
+
+**SPECULATIVE (proposal; moderate feasibility for a toy construction, low for a new rigorous asymptotic bound).** Use the computable Jacobi/fruitfulness row where the notes establish it. Generate relations while tracking whether that row leaves the parity rowspace. Seek a sampling rule invariant under hidden CRT sign changes, with coefficients small enough to retain an NFS-like advantage. Global equidistribution of roots is stronger than needed; invariance within a fibre is the relevant premise.
+
+**PROVED (first lemma and measurable target).** Equation (9.1) converts fruitful-dependency search into one rank comparison, and the fibre calculation supplies the success law if its symmetry premise holds. The next necessary lemma is quantitative: the proposed small-coefficient relation sampler produces the rank increase at a stated frequency after conditioning on the visible transcript. Merely observing a large kernel does not establish it.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (kill criterion).** Stop if the fruitfulness row always lies in the parity rowspace for the selected relation family, if sign-sensitive filtering destroys the fibre argument, or if enforcing the symmetry expands coefficients to the Dixon scale and erases the intended gain. Charge relation supply and descent separately; a fast linear-algebra stage cannot compensate for absent useful relations.
+
+### Rank 4 — prove a residual-tail theorem for a deterministic order-exposure portfolio
+
+**SPECULATIVE (proposal; high feasibility for finite falsification, moderate for an average-case lemma, low for worst case).** Fix inexpensive Pollard-$p-1$ and fixed-curve ECM tests, then use D′ or a deterministic fallback on unexposed inputs. Count prime-signature collisions and the residual semiprime mass, rather than the marginal success probability of a single test. The finite task is to identify which residual patterns survive every test; the mathematical task is a uniform bound on their mass.
+
+**PROVED (first quantitative lemma).** If the initial stage costs at most $N^a$, its residual fraction is $\eta$, and the fallback costs at most $N^b$, the mean is at most $N^a+\eta N^b$. With $b=1/5$, this bound certifies a target mean $N^{1/6-\delta+o(1)}$ when $a\le1/6-\delta$ and
+
+$$
+\eta\le N^{-1/30-\delta+o(1)}.
+$$
+
+A fixed positive proportion of successful order tests cannot certify this exponent by the displayed estimate alone. A faster conditional fallback mean could alter the estimate. Equal-signature classes satisfy the exact pair-count constraint in §8; their empirical histogram is a useful diagnostic but is not a proof of a tail bound.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (kill criterion).** Kill a claimed mean-exponent gain if the surviving fraction stays bounded away from zero, if it is only known to be $o(1)$ with no rate, or if the fallback distribution has not been bounded. An exceptionally difficult finite residual family is useful evidence for redesigning the portfolio, not an asymptotic lower bound.
+
+### Rank 5 — test whether batched large-prime-factor search beats enumeration
+
+**SPECULATIVE (proposal; high feasibility for a decisive cost audit, low for a positive construction).** Seek a batched predicate that locates a large prime factor somewhere in a short interval without separately factoring every integer. This is the missing ingredient in the prime-construction route, rather than a better individual factorizer. Require ordinary binary inputs and charge the formation and reading of every product.
+
+**PROVED (first lemma and threshold).** If a theorem guarantees a factor $>X^\theta$, and the **entire** search and extraction costs $X^{b+o(1)}$, the resulting prime has lower scale $z=X^\theta$, time $z^{b/\theta+o(1)}$, and upper scale $z^{1/\theta+o(1)}$. To beat the $z^{1/2}$ baseline one needs $b<\theta/2$. If extraction alone costs $X^\alpha$, and batching costs $X^\xi$, the necessary condition is $\max(\xi,\alpha)<\theta/2$, rather than $\beta+\alpha<\theta/2$. This separates the batching target from the enumeration calculation in §4.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (kill criterion).** A materialized product of $X^\beta$ integers already has $\Theta(X^\beta\log X)$ bits. For $\beta\ge1/2$, $\theta\le1$, merely reading that product prevents the desired strict inequality. A factoring oracle for succinct straight-line-program inputs is a different hypothesis from H1; any positive proposal using one must prove that stronger interface.
+
+## 11. Verification and scope of the answer
+
+**PROVED (finite verification record).** Exact rational arithmetic checks all four rows of the interval-exponent table. Direct enumeration for $2\le N\le2000$ checks the totient-to-radical and divisor-count-to-μ constructions. For odd primes below 80 and $1\le k\le8$, all residues modulo $2^k$ agree with the stated 2-adic root classification. Binomial divisibility is checked through $k=119$. These checks catch arithmetic errors; the preceding arguments provide the proofs. All 26 repository links resolve to existing files.
+
+**PROVED (what is and is not claimed).** The reductions with full proofs here are elementary consequences of the stated interfaces. The combined theorem is a restricted-model counting result with explicit key and signature premises. Neither it nor any cited geometric, generic-ring, or bounded-depth result is a lower bound for unrestricted classical bit algorithms. The short-interval arithmetic establishes the failure of the checked routes to improve prime construction; it does not prove that H1 can never have such a consequence through a future reduction.
+
+**SPECULATIVE (remaining literature gaps).** The Haugland attribution is recorded at abstract level, not upgraded to a checked numbered theorem. The precise historical relativized μ/squarefreeness result requested from Woll was not verified, and no such separation is asserted. Bach’s original numbered abstract statements are distinguished from theorem numbers in its scanned text. These gaps limit the historical catalog; none is used as a premise of the proved reductions or combined theorem.
+
+**PROVED (recommended interpretation).** A candidate factorizer deserves an independent blocker only after its mechanism implies the additional open statement. Fast factoring by itself supplies no contradiction with the analytic or complexity results audited here. The useful obligations are concrete: a global selector with affordable placement, a quantified deterministic order-supply tail, a promise-preserving partial-bit reduction, or a relation sampler with proved supply and fruitfulness.

